-- NRSV-CE OLD TESTAMENT BIBLE VERSES (PART 9)

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 1, 'In the second year of the reign of Ahasuerus the Great, on the first day of Nisan, Mordecai son of Jair son of Shimei son of Kish, of the tribe of Benjamin, had a dream. He was a Jew living in the city of Susa, a great man, serving in the court of the king. He was one of the captives whom King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon had brought from Jerusalem with King Jeconiah of Judah. And this was his dream: Noises and confusion, thunders and earthquake, tumult on the earth! Then two great dragons came forward, both ready to fight, and they roared terribly. At their roaring every nation prepared for war, to fight against the righteous nation. It was a day of darkness and gloom, of tribulation and distress, affliction and great tumult on the earth! And the whole righteous nation was troubled; they feared the evils that threatened them, and were ready to perish. Then they cried out to God; and at their outcry, as though from a tiny spring, there came a great river, with abundant water; light came, and the sun rose, and the lowly were exalted and devoured those held in honor.Mordecai saw in this dream what God had determined to do, and after he awoke he had it on his mind, seeking all day to understand it in every detail.Now Mordecai took his rest in the courtyard with Bigthan and Teresh, the two eunuchs of the king who kept watch in the courtyard. He overheard their conversation and inquired into their purposes, and learned that they were preparing to lay hands on King Ahasuerus; and he informed the king concerning them. Then the king examined the two eunuchs, and after they had confessed it, they were led away to execution. The king made a permanent record of these things, and Mordecai wrote an account of them. And the king ordered Mordecai to serve in the court, and rewarded him for these things. But Haman son of Hammedatha, the Agagite, who was in great honor with the king, determined to injure Mordecai and his people because of the two eunuchs of the king.This happened in the days of Ahasuerus, the same Ahasuerus who ruled over one hundred twenty-seven provinces from India to Ethiopia.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Mordecai"s Dream'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 2, 'In those days when King Ahasuerus sat on his royal throne in the citadel of Susa,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 3, 'in the third year of his reign, he gave a banquet for all his officials and ministers. The army of Persia and Media and the nobles and governors of the provinces were present,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 4, 'while he displayed the great wealth of his kingdom and the splendor and pomp of his majesty for many days, one hundred eighty days in all.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 5, 'When these days were completed, the king gave for all the people present in the citadel of Susa, both great and small, a banquet lasting for seven days, in the court of the garden of the king"s palace.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 6, 'There were white cotton curtains and blue hangings tied with cords of fine linen and purple to silver rings and marble pillars. There were couches of gold and silver on a mosaic pavement of porphyry, marble, mother-of-pearl, and colored stones.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 7, 'Drinks were served in golden goblets, goblets of different kinds, and the royal wine was lavished according to the bounty of the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 8, 'Drinking was by flagons, without restraint; for the king had given orders to all the officials of his palace to do as each one desired.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 9, 'Furthermore, Queen Vashti gave a banquet for the women in the palace of King Ahasuerus.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 10, 'On the seventh day, when the king was merry with wine, he commanded Mehuman, Biztha, Harbona, Bigtha and Abagtha, Zethar and Carkas, the seven eunuchs who attended him,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 11, 'to bring Queen Vashti before the king, wearing the royal crown, in order to show the peoples and the officials her beauty; for she was fair to behold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 12, 'But Queen Vashti refused to come at the king"s command conveyed by the eunuchs. At this the king was enraged, and his anger burned within him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 13, 'Then the king consulted the sages who knew the laws (for this was the king"s procedure toward all who were versed in law and custom,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 14, 'and those next to him were Carshena, Shethar, Admatha, Tarshish, Meres, Marsena, and Memucan, the seven officials of Persia and Media, who had access to the king, and sat first in the kingdom):', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 15, '"According to the law, what is to be done to Queen Vashti because she has not performed the command of King Ahasuerus conveyed by the eunuchs?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 16, 'Then Memucan said in the presence of the king and the officials, "Not only has Queen Vashti done wrong to the king, but also to all the officials and all the peoples who are in all the provinces of King Ahasuerus.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 17, 'For this deed of the queen will be made known to all women, causing them to look with contempt on their husbands, since they will say, "King Ahasuerus commanded Queen Vashti to be brought before him, and she did not come."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 18, 'This very day the noble ladies of Persia and Media who have heard of the queen"s behavior will rebel against the king"s officials, and there will be no end of contempt and wrath!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 19, 'If it pleases the king, let a royal order go out from him, and let it be written among the laws of the Persians and the Medes so that it may not be altered, that Vashti is never again to come before King Ahasuerus; and let the king give her royal position to another who is better than she.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 20, 'So when the decree made by the king is proclaimed throughout all his kingdom, vast as it is, all women will give honor to their husbands, high and low alike."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 21, 'This advice pleased the king and the officials, and the king did as Memucan proposed;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 1, 22, 'he sent letters to all the royal provinces, to every province in its own script and to every people in its own language, declaring that every man should be master in his own house.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 1, 'After these things, when the anger of King Ahasuerus had abated, he remembered Vashti and what she had done and what had been decreed against her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Esther Becomes Queen'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 2, 'Then the king"s servants who attended him said, "Let beautiful young virgins be sought out for the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 3, 'And let the king appoint commissioners in all the provinces of his kingdom to gather all the beautiful young virgins to the harem in the citadel of Susa under custody of Hegai, the king"s eunuch, who is in charge of the women; let their cosmetic treatments be given them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 4, 'And let the girl who pleases the king be queen instead of Vashti." This pleased the king, and he did so.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 5, 'Now there was a Jew in the citadel of Susa whose name was Mordecai son of Jair son of Shimei son of Kish, a Benjaminite.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 6, 'Kish had been carried away from Jerusalem among the captives carried away with King Jeconiah of Judah, whom King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon had carried away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 7, 'Mordecai had brought up Hadassah, that is Esther, his cousin, for she had neither father nor mother; the girl was fair and beautiful, and when her father and her mother died, Mordecai adopted her as his own daughter.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 8, 'So when the king"s order and his edict were proclaimed, and when many young women were gathered in the citadel of Susa in custody of Hegai, Esther also was taken into the king"s palace and put in custody of Hegai, who had charge of the women.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 9, 'The girl pleased him and won his favor, and he quickly provided her with her cosmetic treatments and her portion of food, and with seven chosen maids from the king"s palace, and advanced her and her maids to the best place in the harem.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 10, 'Esther did not reveal her people or kindred, for Mordecai had charged her not to tell.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 11, 'Every day Mordecai would walk around in front of the court of the harem, to learn how Esther was and how she fared.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 12, 'The turn came for each girl to go in to King Ahasuerus, after being twelve months under the regulations for the women, since this was the regular period of their cosmetic treatment, six months with oil of myrrh and six months with perfumes and cosmetics for women.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 13, 'When the girl went in to the king she was given whatever she asked for to take with her from the harem to the king"s palace.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 14, 'In the evening she went in; then in the morning she came back to the second harem in custody of Shaashgaz, the king"s eunuch, who was in charge of the concubines; she did not go in to the king again, unless the king delighted in her and she was summoned by name.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 15, 'When the turn came for Esther daughter of Abihail the uncle of Mordecai, who had adopted her as his own daughter, to go in to the king, she asked for nothing except what Hegai the king"s eunuch, who had charge of the women, advised. Now Esther was admired by all who saw her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 16, 'When Esther was taken to King Ahasuerus in his royal palace in the tenth month, which is the month of Tebeth, in the seventh year of his reign,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 17, 'the king loved Esther more than all the other women; of all the virgins she won his favor and devotion, so that he set the royal crown on her head and made her queen instead of Vashti.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 18, 'Then the king gave a great banquet to all his officials and ministers--"Esther"s banquet." He also granted a holiday to the provinces, and gave gifts with royal liberality.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 19, 'When the virgins were being gathered together, Mordecai was sitting at the king"s gate.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Mordecai Discovers a Plot'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 20, 'Now Esther had not revealed her kindred or her people, as Mordecai had charged her; for Esther obeyed Mordecai just as when she was brought up by him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 21, 'In those days, while Mordecai was sitting at the king"s gate, Bigthan and Teresh, two of the king"s eunuchs, who guarded the threshold, became angry and conspired to assassinate King Ahasuerus.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 22, 'But the matter came to the knowledge of Mordecai, and he told it to Queen Esther, and Esther told the king in the name of Mordecai.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 2, 23, 'When the affair was investigated and found to be so, both the men were hanged on the gallows. It was recorded in the book of the annals in the presence of the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 1, 'After these things King Ahasuerus promoted Haman son of Hammedatha the Agagite, and advanced him and set his seat above all the officials who were with him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Haman Undertakes to Destroy the Jews'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 2, 'And all the king"s servants who were at the king"s gate bowed down and did obeisance to Haman; for the king had so commanded concerning him. But Mordecai did not bow down or do obeisance.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 3, 'Then the king"s servants who were at the king"s gate said to Mordecai, "Why do you disobey the king"s command?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 4, 'When they spoke to him day after day and he would not listen to them, they told Haman, in order to see whether Mordecai"s words would avail; for he had told them that he was a Jew.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 5, 'When Haman saw that Mordecai did not bow down or do obeisance to him, Haman was infuriated.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 6, 'But he thought it beneath him to lay hands on Mordecai alone. So, having been told who Mordecai"s people were, Haman plotted to destroy all the Jews, the people of Mordecai, throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 7, 'In the first month, which is the month of Nisan, in the twelfth year of King Ahasuerus, they cast Pur--which means "the lot"--before Haman for the day and for the month, and the lot fell on the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, which is the month of Adar.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 8, 'Then Haman said to King Ahasuerus, "There is a certain people scattered and separated among the peoples in all the provinces of your kingdom; their laws are different from those of every other people, and they do not keep the king"s laws, so that it is not appropriate for the king to tolerate them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 9, 'If it pleases the king, let a decree be issued for their destruction, and I will pay ten thousand talents of silver into the hands of those who have charge of the king"s business, so that they may put it into the king"s treasuries."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 10, 'So the king took his signet ring from his hand and gave it to Haman son of Hammedatha the Agagite, the enemy of the Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 11, 'The king said to Haman, "The money is given to you, and the people as well, to do with them as it seems good to you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 12, 'Then the king"s secretaries were summoned on the thirteenth day of the first month, and an edict, according to all that Haman commanded, was written to the king"s satraps and to the governors over all the provinces and to the officials of all the peoples, to every province in its own script and every people in its own language; it was written in the name of King Ahasuerus and sealed with the king"s ring.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 13, 'Letters were sent by couriers to all the king"s provinces, giving orders to destroy, to kill, and to annihilate all Jews, young and old, women and children, in one day, the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, which is the month of Adar, and to plunder their goods. This is a copy of the letter: "The Great King, Ahasuerus, writes the following to the governors of the hundred twenty-seven provinces from India to Ethiopia and to the officials under them:"Having become ruler of many nations and master of the whole world (not elated with presumption of authority but always acting reasonably and with kindness), I have determined to settle the lives of my subjects in lasting tranquility and, in order to make my kingdom peaceable and open to travel throughout all its extent, to restore the peace desired by all people."When I asked my counselors how this might be accomplished, Haman--who excels among us in sound judgment, and is distinguished for his unchanging goodwill and steadfast fidelity, and has attained the second place in the kingdom-- pointed out to us that among all the nations in the world there is scattered a certain hostile people, who have laws contrary to those of every nation and continually disregard the ordinances of kings, so that the unifying of the kingdom that we honorably intend cannot be brought about. We understand that this people, and it alone, stands constantly in opposition to every nation, perversely following a strange manner of life and laws, and is ill-disposed to our government, doing all the harm they can so that our kingdom may not attain stability."Therefore we have decreed that those indicated to you in the letters written by Haman, who is in charge of affairs and is our second father, shall all--wives and children included--be utterly destroyed by the swords of their enemies, without pity or restraint, on the fourteenth day of the twelfth month, Adar, of this present year, so that those who have long been hostile and remain so may in a single day go down in violence to Hades, and leave our government completely secure and untroubled hereafter."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'The King"s Letter'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 14, 'A copy of the document was to be issued as a decree in every province by proclamation, calling on all the peoples to be ready for that day.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 3, 15, 'The couriers went quickly by order of the king, and the decree was issued in the citadel of Susa. The king and Haman sat down to drink; but the city of Susa was thrown into confusion.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 1, 'When Mordecai learned all that had been done, Mordecai tore his clothes and put on sackcloth and ashes, and went through the city, wailing with a loud and bitter cry;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Esther Agrees to Help the Jews'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 2, 'he went up to the entrance of the king"s gate, for no one might enter the king"s gate clothed with sackcloth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 3, 'In every province, wherever the king"s command and his decree came, there was great mourning among the Jews, with fasting and weeping and lamenting, and most of them lay in sackcloth and ashes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 4, 'When Esther"s maids and her eunuchs came and told her, the queen was deeply distressed; she sent garments to clothe Mordecai, so that he might take off his sackcloth; but he would not accept them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 5, 'Then Esther called for Hathach, one of the king"s eunuchs, who had been appointed to attend her, and ordered him to go to Mordecai to learn what was happening and why.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 6, 'Hathach went out to Mordecai in the open square of the city in front of the king"s gate,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 7, 'and Mordecai told him all that had happened to him, and the exact sum of money that Haman had promised to pay into the king"s treasuries for the destruction of the Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 8, 'Mordecai also gave him a copy of the written decree issued in Susa for their destruction, that he might show it to Esther, explain it to her, and charge her to go to the king to make supplication to him and entreat him for her people.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 9, 'Hathach went and told Esther what Mordecai had said.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 10, 'Then Esther spoke to Hathach and gave him a message for Mordecai, saying,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 11, '"All the king"s servants and the people of the king"s provinces know that if any man or woman goes to the king inside the inner court without being called, there is but one law--all alike are to be put to death. Only if the king holds out the golden scepter to someone, may that person live. I myself have not been called to come in to the king for thirty days."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 12, 'When they told Mordecai what Esther had said,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 13, 'Mordecai told them to reply to Esther, "Do not think that in the king"s palace you will escape any more than all the other Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 14, 'For if you keep silence at such a time as this, relief and deliverance will rise for the Jews from another quarter, but you and your father"s family will perish. Who knows? Perhaps you have come to royal dignity for just such a time as this."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 15, 'Then Esther said in reply to Mordecai,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 16, '"Go, gather all the Jews to be found in Susa, and hold a fast on my behalf, and neither eat nor drink for three days, night or day. I and my maids will also fast as you do. After that I will go to the king, though it is against the law; and if I perish, I perish."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 4, 17, 'Mordecai then went away and did everything as Esther had ordered him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 1, 'Then Mordecai prayed to the Lord, calling to remembrance all the works of the Lord.He said, "O Lord, Lord, you rule as King over all things, for the universe is in your power and there is no one who can oppose you when it is your will to save Israel, for you have made heaven and earth and every wonderful thing under heaven. You are Lord of all, and there is no one who can resist you, the Lord. You know all things; you know, O Lord, that it was not in insolence or pride or for any love of glory that I did this, and refused to bow down to this proud Haman; for I would have been willing to kiss the soles of his feet to save Israel! But I did this so that I might not set human glory above the glory of God, and I will not bow down to anyone but you, who are my Lord; and I will not do these things in pride. And now, O Lord God and King, God of Abraham, spare your people; for the eyes of our foes are upon us to annihilate us, and they desire to destroy the inheritance that has been yours from the beginning. Do not neglect your portion, which you redeemed for yourself out of the land of Egypt. Hear my prayer, and have mercy upon your inheritance; turn our mourning into feasting that we may live and sing praise to your name, O Lord; do not destroy the lips of those who praise you."And all Israel cried out mightily, for their death was before their eyes.Then Queen Esther, seized with deadly anxiety, fled to the Lord. She took off her splendid apparel and put on the garments of distress and mourning, and instead of costly perfumes she covered her head with ashes and dung, and she utterly humbled her body; every part that she loved to adorn she covered with her tangled hair. She prayed to the Lord God of Israel, and said: "O my Lord, you only are our king; help me, who am alone and have no helper but you, for my danger is in my hand. Ever since I was born I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Lord, took Israel out of all the nations, and our ancestors from among all their forebears, for an everlasting inheritance, and that you did for them all that you promised. And now we have sinned before you, and you have handed us over to our enemies because we glorified their gods. You are righteous, O Lord! And now they are not satisfied that we are in bitter slavery, but they have covenanted with their idols to abolish what your mouth has ordained, and to destroy your inheritance, to stop the mouths of those who praise you and to quench your altar and the glory of your house, to open the mouths of the nations for the praise of vain idols, and to magnify forever a mortal king."O Lord, do not surrender your scepter to what has no being; and do not let them laugh at our downfall; but turn their plan against them, and make an example of him who began this against us. Remember, O Lord; make yourself known in this time of our affliction, and give me courage, O King of the gods and Master of all dominion! Put eloquent speech in my mouth before the lion, and turn his heart to hate the man who is fighting against us, so that there may be an end of him and those who agree with him. But save us by your hand, and help me, who am alone and have no helper but you, O Lord. You have knowledge of all things, and you know that I hate the splendor of the wicked and abhor the bed of the uncircumcised and of any alien. You know my necessity--that I abhor the sign of my proud position, which is upon my head on days when I appear in public. I abhor it like a filthy rag, and I do not wear it on the days when I am at leisure. And your servant has not eaten at Haman"s table, and I have not honored the king"s feast or drunk the wine of libations. Your servant has had no joy since the day that I was brought here until now, except in you, O Lord God of Abraham. O God, whose might is over all, hear the voice of the despairing, and save us from the hands of evildoers. And save me from my fear!"On the third day, when she ended her prayer, she took off the garments in which she had worshiped, and arrayed herself in splendid attire. Then, majestically adorned, after invoking the aid of the all-seeing God and Savior, she took two maids with her; on one she leaned gently for support, while the other followed, carrying her train. She was radiant with perfect beauty, and she looked happy, as if beloved, but her heart was frozen with fear. When she had gone through all the doors, she stood before the king. He was seated on his royal throne, clothed in the full array of his majesty, all covered with gold and precious stones. He was most terrifying.Lifting his face, flushed with splendor, he looked at her in fierce anger. The queen faltered, and turned pale and faint, and collapsed on the head of the maid who went in front of her. Then God changed the spirit of the king to gentleness, and in alarm he sprang from his throne and took her in his arms until she came to herself. He comforted her with soothing words, and said to her, "What is it, Esther? I am your husband. Take courage; You shall not die, for our law applies only to our subjects. Come near."Then he raised the golden scepter and touched her neck with it; he embraced her, and said, "Speak to me." She said to him, "I saw you, my lord, like an angel of God, and my heart was shaken with fear at your glory. For you are wonderful, my lord, and your countenance is full of grace." And while she was speaking, she fainted and fell. Then the king was agitated, and all his servants tried to comfort her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Mordecai"s Prayer'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 2, 'Then Mordecai prayed to the Lord, calling to remembrance all the works of the Lord.He said, "O Lord, Lord, you rule as King over all things, for the universe is in your power and there is no one who can oppose you when it is your will to save Israel, for you have made heaven and earth and every wonderful thing under heaven. You are Lord of all, and there is no one who can resist you, the Lord. You know all things; you know, O Lord, that it was not in insolence or pride or for any love of glory that I did this, and refused to bow down to this proud Haman; for I would have been willing to kiss the soles of his feet to save Israel! But I did this so that I might not set human glory above the glory of God, and I will not bow down to anyone but you, who are my Lord; and I will not do these things in pride. And now, O Lord God and King, God of Abraham, spare your people; for the eyes of our foes are upon us to annihilate us, and they desire to destroy the inheritance that has been yours from the beginning. Do not neglect your portion, which you redeemed for yourself out of the land of Egypt. Hear my prayer, and have mercy upon your inheritance; turn our mourning into feasting that we may live and sing praise to your name, O Lord; do not destroy the lips of those who praise you."And all Israel cried out mightily, for their death was before their eyes.Then Queen Esther, seized with deadly anxiety, fled to the Lord. She took off her splendid apparel and put on the garments of distress and mourning, and instead of costly perfumes she covered her head with ashes and dung, and she utterly humbled her body; every part that she loved to adorn she covered with her tangled hair. She prayed to the Lord God of Israel, and said: "O my Lord, you only are our king; help me, who am alone and have no helper but you, for my danger is in my hand. Ever since I was born I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Lord, took Israel out of all the nations, and our ancestors from among all their forebears, for an everlasting inheritance, and that you did for them all that you promised. And now we have sinned before you, and you have handed us over to our enemies because we glorified their gods. You are righteous, O Lord! And now they are not satisfied that we are in bitter slavery, but they have covenanted with their idols to abolish what your mouth has ordained, and to destroy your inheritance, to stop the mouths of those who praise you and to quench your altar and the glory of your house, to open the mouths of the nations for the praise of vain idols, and to magnify forever a mortal king."O Lord, do not surrender your scepter to what has no being; and do not let them laugh at our downfall; but turn their plan against them, and make an example of him who began this against us. Remember, O Lord; make yourself known in this time of our affliction, and give me courage, O King of the gods and Master of all dominion! Put eloquent speech in my mouth before the lion, and turn his heart to hate the man who is fighting against us, so that there may be an end of him and those who agree with him. But save us by your hand, and help me, who am alone and have no helper but you, O Lord. You have knowledge of all things, and you know that I hate the splendor of the wicked and abhor the bed of the uncircumcised and of any alien. You know my necessity--that I abhor the sign of my proud position, which is upon my head on days when I appear in public. I abhor it like a filthy rag, and I do not wear it on the days when I am at leisure. And your servant has not eaten at Haman"s table, and I have not honored the king"s feast or drunk the wine of libations. Your servant has had no joy since the day that I was brought here until now, except in you, O Lord God of Abraham. O God, whose might is over all, hear the voice of the despairing, and save us from the hands of evildoers. And save me from my fear!"On the third day, when she ended her prayer, she took off the garments in which she had worshiped, and arrayed herself in splendid attire. Then, majestically adorned, after invoking the aid of the all-seeing God and Savior, she took two maids with her; on one she leaned gently for support, while the other followed, carrying her train. She was radiant with perfect beauty, and she looked happy, as if beloved, but her heart was frozen with fear. When she had gone through all the doors, she stood before the king. He was seated on his royal throne, clothed in the full array of his majesty, all covered with gold and precious stones. He was most terrifying.Lifting his face, flushed with splendor, he looked at her in fierce anger. The queen faltered, and turned pale and faint, and collapsed on the head of the maid who went in front of her. Then God changed the spirit of the king to gentleness, and in alarm he sprang from his throne and took her in his arms until she came to herself. He comforted her with soothing words, and said to her, "What is it, Esther? I am your husband. Take courage; You shall not die, for our law applies only to our subjects. Come near."Then he raised the golden scepter and touched her neck with it; he embraced her, and said, "Speak to me." She said to him, "I saw you, my lord, like an angel of God, and my heart was shaken with fear at your glory. For you are wonderful, my lord, and your countenance is full of grace." And while she was speaking, she fainted and fell. Then the king was agitated, and all his servants tried to comfort her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Mordecai"s Prayer'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 3, 'The king said to her, "What is it, Queen Esther? What is your request? It shall be given you, even to the half of my kingdom."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 4, 'Then Esther said, "If it pleases the king, let the king and Haman come today to a banquet that I have prepared for the king."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 5, 'Then the king said, "Bring Haman quickly, so that we may do as Esther desires." So the king and Haman came to the banquet that Esther had prepared.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 6, 'While they were drinking wine, the king said to Esther, "What is your petition? It shall be granted you. And what is your request? Even to the half of my kingdom, it shall be fulfilled."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 7, 'Then Esther said, "This is my petition and request:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 8, 'If I have won the king"s favor, and if it pleases the king to grant my petition and fulfill my request, let the king and Haman come tomorrow to the banquet that I will prepare for them, and then I will do as the king has said."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 9, 'Haman went out that day happy and in good spirits. But when Haman saw Mordecai in the king"s gate, and observed that he neither rose nor trembled before him, he was infuriated with Mordecai;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Haman Plans to Have Mordecai Hanged'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 10, 'nevertheless Haman restrained himself and went home. Then he sent and called for his friends and his wife Zeresh,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 11, 'and Haman recounted to them the splendor of his riches, the number of his sons, all the promotions with which the king had honored him, and how he had advanced him above the officials and the ministers of the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 12, 'Haman added, "Even Queen Esther let no one but myself come with the king to the banquet that she prepared. Tomorrow also I am invited by her, together with the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 13, 'Yet all this does me no good so long as I see the Jew Mordecai sitting at the king"s gate."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 5, 14, 'Then his wife Zeresh and all his friends said to him, "Let a gallows fifty cubits high be made, and in the morning tell the king to have Mordecai hanged on it; then go with the king to the banquet in good spirits." This advice pleased Haman, and he had the gallows made.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 1, 'On that night the king could not sleep, and he gave orders to bring the book of records, the annals, and they were read to the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'The King Honors Mordecai'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 2, 'It was found written how Mordecai had told about Bigthana and Teresh, two of the king"s eunuchs, who guarded the threshold, and who had conspired to assassinate King Ahasuerus.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 3, 'Then the king said, "What honor or distinction has been bestowed on Mordecai for this?" The king"s servants who attended him said, "Nothing has been done for him."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 4, 'The king said, "Who is in the court?" Now Haman had just entered the outer court of the king"s palace to speak to the king about having Mordecai hanged on the gallows that he had prepared for him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 5, 'So the king"s servants told him, "Haman is there, standing in the court." The king said, "Let him come in."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 6, 'So Haman came in, and the king said to him, "What shall be done for the man whom the king wishes to honor?" Haman said to himself, "Whom would the king wish to honor more than me?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 7, 'So Haman said to the king, "For the man whom the king wishes to honor,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 8, 'let royal robes be brought, which the king has worn, and a horse that the king has ridden, with a royal crown on its head.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 9, 'Let the robes and the horse be handed over to one of the king"s most noble officials; let him robe the man whom the king wishes to honor, and let him conduct the man on horseback through the open square of the city, proclaiming before him: "Thus shall it be done for the man whom the king wishes to honor.""', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 10, 'Then the king said to Haman, "Quickly, take the robes and the horse, as you have said, and do so to the Jew Mordecai who sits at the king"s gate. Leave out nothing that you have mentioned."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 11, 'So Haman took the robes and the horse and robed Mordecai and led him riding through the open square of the city, proclaiming, "Thus shall it be done for the man whom the king wishes to honor."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 12, 'Then Mordecai returned to the king"s gate, but Haman hurried to his house, mourning and with his head covered.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 13, 'When Haman told his wife Zeresh and all his friends everything that had happened to him, his advisers and his wife Zeresh said to him, "If Mordecai, before whom your downfall has begun, is of the Jewish people, you will not prevail against him, but will surely fall before him."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 6, 14, 'While they were still talking with him, the king"s eunuchs arrived and hurried Haman off to the banquet that Esther had prepared.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Haman"s Downfall and Mordecai"s Advancement');

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 1, 'So the king and Haman went in to feast with Queen Esther.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 2, 'On the second day, as they were drinking wine, the king again said to Esther, "What is your petition, Queen Esther? It shall be granted you. And what is your request? Even to the half of my kingdom, it shall be fulfilled."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 3, 'Then Queen Esther answered, "If I have won your favor, O king, and if it pleases the king, let my life be given me--that is my petition--and the lives of my people--that is my request.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 4, 'For we have been sold, I and my people, to be destroyed, to be killed, and to be annihilated. If we had been sold merely as slaves, men and women, I would have held my peace; but no enemy can compensate for this damage to the king."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 5, 'Then King Ahasuerus said to Queen Esther, "Who is he, and where is he, who has presumed to do this?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 6, 'Esther said, "A foe and enemy, this wicked Haman!" Then Haman was terrified before the king and the queen.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 7, 'The king rose from the feast in wrath and went into the palace garden, but Haman stayed to beg his life from Queen Esther, for he saw that the king had determined to destroy him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 8, 'When the king returned from the palace garden to the banquet hall, Haman had thrown himself on the couch where Esther was reclining; and the king said, "Will he even assault the queen in my presence, in my own house?" As the words left the mouth of the king, they covered Haman"s face.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 9, 'Then Harbona, one of the eunuchs in attendance on the king, said, "Look, the very gallows that Haman has prepared for Mordecai, whose word saved the king, stands at Haman"s house, fifty cubits high." And the king said, "Hang him on that."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 7, 10, 'So they hanged Haman on the gallows that he had prepared for Mordecai. Then the anger of the king abated.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 1, 'On that day King Ahasuerus gave to Queen Esther the house of Haman, the enemy of the Jews; and Mordecai came before the king, for Esther had told what he was to her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Esther Saves the Jews'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 2, 'Then the king took off his signet ring, which he had taken from Haman, and gave it to Mordecai. So Esther set Mordecai over the house of Haman.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 3, 'Then Esther spoke again to the king; she fell at his feet, weeping and pleading with him to avert the evil design of Haman the Agagite and the plot that he had devised against the Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 4, 'The king held out the golden scepter to Esther,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 5, 'and Esther rose and stood before the king. She said, "If it pleases the king, and if I have won his favor, and if the thing seems right before the king, and I have his approval, let an order be written to revoke the letters devised by Haman son of Hammedatha the Agagite, which he wrote giving orders to destroy the Jews who are in all the provinces of the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 6, 'For how can I bear to see the calamity that is coming on my people? Or how can I bear to see the destruction of my kindred?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 7, 'Then King Ahasuerus said to Queen Esther and to the Jew Mordecai, "See, I have given Esther the house of Haman, and they have hanged him on the gallows, because he plotted to lay hands on the Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 8, 'You may write as you please with regard to the Jews, in the name of the king, and seal it with the king"s ring; for an edict written in the name of the king and sealed with the king"s ring cannot be revoked."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 9, 'The king"s secretaries were summoned at that time, in the third month, which is the month of Sivan, on the twenty-third day; and an edict was written, according to all that Mordecai commanded, to the Jews and to the satraps and the governors and the officials of the provinces from India to Ethiopia, one hundred twenty-seven provinces, to every province in its own script and to every people in its own language, and also to the Jews in their script and their language.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 10, 'He wrote letters in the name of King Ahasuerus, sealed them with the king"s ring, and sent them by mounted couriers riding on fast steeds bred from the royal herd.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 11, 'By these letters the king allowed the Jews who were in every city to assemble and defend their lives, to destroy, to kill, and to annihilate any armed force of any people or province that might attack them, with their children and women, and to plunder their goods', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 12, 'on a single day throughout all the provinces of King Ahasuerus, on the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, which is the month of Adar. The following is a copy of this letter:
"The Great King, Ahasuerus, to the governors of the provinces from India to Ethiopia, one hundred twenty-seven provinces, and to those who are loyal to our government, greetings."Many people, the more they are honored with the most generous kindness of their benefactors, the more proud do they become, and not only seek to injure our subjects, but in their inability to stand prosperity, they even undertake to scheme against their own benefactors. They not only take away thankfulness from others, but, carried away by the boasts of those who know nothing of goodness, they even assume that they will escape the evil-hating justice of God, who always sees everything. And often many of those who are set in places of authority have been made in part responsible for the shedding of innocent blood, and have been involved in irremediable calamities, by the persuasion of friends who have been entrusted with the administration of public affairs, when these persons by the false trickery of their evil natures beguile the sincere goodwill of their sovereigns."What has been wickedly accomplished through the pestilent behavior of those who exercise authority unworthily can be seen, not so much from the more ancient records that we hand on, as from investigation of matters close at hand. In the future we will take care to render our kingdom quiet and peaceable for all, by changing our methods and always judging what comes before our eyes with more equitable consideration. For Haman son of Hammedatha, a Macedonian (really an alien to the Persian blood, and quite devoid of our kindliness), having become our guest, enjoyed so fully the goodwill that we have for every nation that he was called our father and was continually bowed down to by all as the person second to the royal throne. But, unable to restrain his arrogance, he undertook to deprive us of our kingdom and our life, and with intricate craft and deceit asked for the destruction of Mordecai, our savior and perpetual benefactor, and of Esther, the blameless partner of our kingdom, together with their whole nation. He thought that by these methods he would catch us undefended and would transfer the kingdom of the Persians to the Macedonians."But we find that the Jews, who were consigned to annihilation by this thrice-accursed man, are not evildoers, but are governed by most righteous laws and are children of the living God, most high, most mighty, who has directed the kingdom both for us and for our ancestors in the most excellent order."You will therefore do well not to put in execution the letters sent by Haman son of Hammedatha, since he, the one who did these things, has been hanged at the gate of Susa with all his household--for God, who rules over all things, has speedily inflicted on him the punishment that he deserved."Therefore post a copy of this letter publicly in every place, and permit the Jews to live under their own laws. And give them reinforcements, so that on the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, Adar, on that very day, they may defend themselves against those who attack them at the time of oppression. For God, who rules over all things, has made this day to be a joy for his chosen people instead of a day of destruction for them."Therefore you shall observe this with all good cheer as a notable day among your commemorative festivals, so that both now and hereafter it may represent deliverance for you and the loyal Persians, but that it may be a reminder of destruction for those who plot against us."Every city and country, without exception, that does not act accordingly shall be destroyed in wrath with spear and fire. It shall be made not only impassable for human beings, but also most hateful to wild animals and birds for all time.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'The Decree of Ahasuerus'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 13, 'A copy of the writ was to be issued as a decree in every province and published to all peoples, and the Jews were to be ready on that day to take revenge on their enemies.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 14, 'So the couriers, mounted on their swift royal steeds, hurried out, urged by the king"s command. The decree was issued in the citadel of Susa.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 15, 'Then Mordecai went out from the presence of the king, wearing royal robes of blue and white, with a great golden crown and a mantle of fine linen and purple, while the city of Susa shouted and rejoiced.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 16, 'For the Jews there was light and gladness, joy and honor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 8, 17, 'In every province and in every city, wherever the king"s command and his edict came, there was gladness and joy among the Jews, a festival and a holiday. Furthermore, many of the peoples of the country professed to be Jews, because the fear of the Jews had fallen upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 1, 'Now in the twelfth month, which is the month of Adar, on the thirteenth day, when the king"s command and edict were about to be executed, on the very day when the enemies of the Jews hoped to gain power over them, but which had been changed to a day when the Jews would gain power over their foes,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Destruction of the Enemies of the Jews'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 2, 'the Jews gathered in their cities throughout all the provinces of King Ahasuerus to lay hands on those who had sought their ruin; and no one could withstand them, because the fear of them had fallen upon all peoples.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 3, 'All the officials of the provinces, the satraps and the governors, and the royal officials were supporting the Jews, because the fear of Mordecai had fallen upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 4, 'For Mordecai was powerful in the king"s house, and his fame spread throughout all the provinces as the man Mordecai grew more and more powerful.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 5, 'So the Jews struck down all their enemies with the sword, slaughtering, and destroying them, and did as they pleased to those who hated them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 6, 'In the citadel of Susa the Jews killed and destroyed five hundred people.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 7, 'They killed Parshandatha, Dalphon, Aspatha,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 8, 'Poratha, Adalia, Aridatha,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 9, 'Parmashta, Arisai, Aridai, Vaizatha,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 10, 'the ten sons of Haman son of Hammedatha, the enemy of the Jews; but they did not touch the plunder.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 11, 'That very day the number of those killed in the citadel of Susa was reported to the king.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 12, 'The king said to Queen Esther, "In the citadel of Susa the Jews have killed five hundred people and also the ten sons of Haman. What have they done in the rest of the king"s provinces? Now what is your petition? It shall be granted you. And what further is your request? It shall be fulfilled."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 13, 'Esther said, "If it pleases the king, let the Jews who are in Susa be allowed tomorrow also to do according to this day"s edict, and let the ten sons of Haman be hanged on the gallows."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 14, 'So the king commanded this to be done; a decree was issued in Susa, and the ten sons of Haman were hanged.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 15, 'The Jews who were in Susa gathered also on the fourteenth day of the month of Adar and they killed three hundred persons in Susa; but they did not touch the plunder.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 16, 'Now the other Jews who were in the king"s provinces also gathered to defend their lives, and gained relief from their enemies, and killed seventy-five thousand of those who hated them; but they laid no hands on the plunder.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 17, 'This was on the thirteenth day of the month of Adar, and on the fourteenth day they rested and made that a day of feasting and gladness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 18, 'But the Jews who were in Susa gathered on the thirteenth day and on the fourteenth, and rested on the fifteenth day, making that a day of feasting and gladness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'The Feast of Purim Inaugurated'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 19, 'Therefore the Jews of the villages, who live in the open towns, hold the fourteenth day of the month of Adar as a day for gladness and feasting, a holiday on which they send gifts of food to one another.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 20, 'Mordecai recorded these things, and sent letters to all the Jews who were in all the provinces of King Ahasuerus, both near and far,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 21, 'enjoining them that they should keep the fourteenth day of the month Adar and also the fifteenth day of the same month, year by year,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 22, 'as the days on which the Jews gained relief from their enemies, and as the month that had been turned for them from sorrow into gladness and from mourning into a holiday; that they should make them days of feasting and gladness, days for sending gifts of food to one another and presents to the poor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 23, 'So the Jews adopted as a custom what they had begun to do, as Mordecai had written to them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 24, 'Haman son of Hammedatha the Agagite, the enemy of all the Jews, had plotted against the Jews to destroy them, and had cast Pur--that is "the lot"--to crush and destroy them;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 25, 'but when Esther came before the king, he gave orders in writing that the wicked plot that he had devised against the Jews should come upon his own head, and that he and his sons should be hanged on the gallows.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 26, 'Therefore these days are called Purim, from the word Pur. Thus because of all that was written in this letter, and of what they had faced in this matter, and of what had happened to them,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 27, 'the Jews established and accepted as a custom for themselves and their descendants and all who joined them, that without fail they would continue to observe these two days every year, as it was written and at the time appointed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 28, 'These days should be remembered and kept throughout every generation, in every family, province, and city; and these days of Purim should never fall into disuse among the Jews, nor should the commemoration of these days cease among their descendants.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 29, 'Queen Esther daughter of Abihail, along with the Jew Mordecai, gave full written authority, confirming this second letter about Purim.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 30, 'Letters were sent wishing peace and security to all the Jews, to the one hundred twenty-seven provinces of the kingdom of Ahasuerus,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 31, 'and giving orders that these days of Purim should be observed at their appointed seasons, as the Jew Mordecai and Queen Esther enjoined on the Jews, just as they had laid down for themselves and for their descendants regulations concerning their fasts and their lamentations.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 9, 32, 'The command of Queen Esther fixed these practices of Purim, and it was recorded in writing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 1, 'King Ahasuerus laid tribute on the land and on the islands of the sea.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 2, 'All the acts of his power and might, and the full account of the high honor of Mordecai, to which the king advanced him, are they not written in the annals of the kings of Media and Persia?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 3, 'For Mordecai the Jew was next in rank to King Ahasuerus, and he was powerful among the Jews and popular with his many kindred, for he sought the good of his people and interceded for the welfare of all his descendants.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 4, 'And Mordecai said, "These things have come from God;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Mordecai"s Dream Fulfilled'),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 5, 'for I remember the dream that I had concerning these matters, and none of them has failed to be fulfilled.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 6, 'There was the little spring that became a river, and there was light and sun and abundant water--the river is Esther, whom the king married and made queen.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 7, 'The two dragons are Haman and myself.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 8, 'The nations are those that gathered to destroy the name of the Jews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 9, 'And my nation, this is Israel, who cried out to God and was saved. The Lord has saved his people; the Lord has rescued us from all these evils; God has done great signs and wonders, wonders that have never happened among the nations.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 10, 'For this purpose he made two lots, one for the people of God and one for all the nations,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 11, 'and these two lots came to the hour and moment and day of decision before God and among all the nations.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 12, 'And God remembered his people and vindicated his inheritance.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(17, 'Est', 'Esther', 10, 13, 'So they will observe these days in the month of Adar, on the fourteenth and fifteenth of that month, with an assembly and joy and gladness before God, from generation to generation forever among his people Israel."In the fourth year of the reign of Ptolemy and Cleopatra, Dositheus, who said that he was a priest and a Levite, and his son Ptolemy brought to Egypt the preceding Letter about Purim, which they said was authentic and had been translated by Lysimachus son of Ptolemy, one of the residents of Jerusalem.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Postscript');

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 1, 'There was once a man in the land of Uz whose name was Job. That man was blameless and upright, one who feared God and turned away from evil.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job and His Family'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 2, 'There were born to him seven sons and three daughters.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 3, 'He had seven thousand sheep, three thousand camels, five hundred yoke of oxen, five hundred donkeys, and very many servants; so that this man was the greatest of all the people of the east.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 4, 'His sons used to go and hold feasts in one another"s houses in turn; and they would send and invite their three sisters to eat and drink with them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 5, 'And when the feast days had run their course, Job would send and sanctify them, and he would rise early in the morning and offer burnt offerings according to the number of them all; for Job said, "It may be that my children have sinned, and cursed God in their hearts." This is what Job always did.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 6, 'One day the heavenly beings came to present themselves before the LORD, and Satan also came among them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Attack on Job"s Character'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 7, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Where have you come from?" Satan answered the LORD, "From going to and fro on the earth, and from walking up and down on it."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 8, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Have you considered my servant Job? There is no one like him on the earth, a blameless and upright man who fears God and turns away from evil."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 9, 'Then Satan answered the LORD, "Does Job fear God for nothing?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 10, 'Have you not put a fence around him and his house and all that he has, on every side? You have blessed the work of his hands, and his possessions have increased in the land.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 11, 'But stretch out your hand now, and touch all that he has, and he will curse you to your face."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 12, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Very well, all that he has is in your power; only do not stretch out your hand against him!" So Satan went out from the presence of the LORD.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 13, 'One day when his sons and daughters were eating and drinking wine in the eldest brother"s house,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Loses Property and Children'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 14, 'a messenger came to Job and said, "The oxen were plowing and the donkeys were feeding beside them,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 15, 'and the Sabeans fell on them and carried them off, and killed the servants with the edge of the sword; I alone have escaped to tell you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 16, 'While he was still speaking, another came and said, "The fire of God fell from heaven and burned up the sheep and the servants, and consumed them; I alone have escaped to tell you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 17, 'While he was still speaking, another came and said, "The Chaldeans formed three columns, made a raid on the camels and carried them off, and killed the servants with the edge of the sword; I alone have escaped to tell you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 18, 'While he was still speaking, another came and said, "Your sons and daughters were eating and drinking wine in their eldest brother"s house,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 19, 'and suddenly a great wind came across the desert, struck the four corners of the house, and it fell on the young people, and they are dead; I alone have escaped to tell you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 20, 'Then Job arose, tore his robe, shaved his head, and fell on the ground and worshiped.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 21, 'He said, "Naked I came from my mother"s womb, and naked shall I return there; the LORD gave, and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 1, 22, 'In all this Job did not sin or charge God with wrongdoing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 1, 'One day the heavenly beings came to present themselves before the LORD, and Satan also came among them to present himself before the LORD.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Attack on Job"s Health'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 2, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Where have you come from?" Satan answered the LORD, "From going to and fro on the earth, and from walking up and down on it."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 3, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Have you considered my servant Job? There is no one like him on the earth, a blameless and upright man who fears God and turns away from evil. He still persists in his integrity, although you incited me against him, to destroy him for no reason."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 4, 'Then Satan answered the LORD, "Skin for skin! All that people have they will give to save their lives.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 5, 'But stretch out your hand now and touch his bone and his flesh, and he will curse you to your face."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 6, 'The LORD said to Satan, "Very well, he is in your power; only spare his life."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 7, 'So Satan went out from the presence of the LORD, and inflicted loathsome sores on Job from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 8, 'Job took a potsherd with which to scrape himself, and sat among the ashes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 9, 'Then his wife said to him, "Do you still persist in your integrity? Curse God, and die."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 10, 'But he said to her, "You speak as any foolish woman would speak. Shall we receive the good at the hand of God, and not receive the bad?" In all this Job did not sin with his lips.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 11, 'Now when Job"s three friends heard of all these troubles that had come upon him, each of them set out from his home--Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite. They met together to go and console and comfort him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job"s Three Friends'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 12, 'When they saw him from a distance, they did not recognize him, and they raised their voices and wept aloud; they tore their robes and threw dust in the air upon their heads.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 2, 13, 'They sat with him on the ground seven days and seven nights, and no one spoke a word to him, for they saw that his suffering was very great.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 1, 'After this Job opened his mouth and cursed the day of his birth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Curses the Day He Was Born'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 2, 'Job said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 3, '"Let the day perish in which I was born,  
    and the night that said,  
    "A man-child is conceived."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 4, 'Let that day be darkness!  
    May God above not seek it,  
    or light shine on it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 5, 'Let gloom and deep darkness claim it.  
    Let clouds settle upon it;  
    let the blackness of the day terrify it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 6, 'That night--let thick darkness seize it!  
    let it not rejoice among the days of the year;  
    let it not come into the number of the months.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 7, 'Yes, let that night be barren;  
    let no joyful cry be heard in it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 8, 'Let those curse it who curse the Sea,  
    those who are skilled to rouse up Leviathan.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 9, 'Let the stars of its dawn be dark;  
    let it hope for light, but have none;  
    may it not see the eyelids of the morning--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 10, 'because it did not shut the doors of my mother"s womb,  
    and hide trouble from my eyes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 11, '"Why did I not die at birth,  
    come forth from the womb and expire?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 12, 'Why were there knees to receive me,  
    or breasts for me to suck?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 13, 'Now I would be lying down and quiet;  
    I would be asleep; then I would be at rest', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 14, 'with kings and counselors of the earth  
    who rebuild ruins for themselves,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 15, 'or with princes who have gold,  
    who fill their houses with silver.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 16, 'Or why was I not buried like a stillborn child,  
    like an infant that never sees the light?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 17, 'There the wicked cease from troubling,  
    and there the weary are at rest.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 18, 'There the prisoners are at ease together;  
    they do not hear the voice of the taskmaster.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 19, 'The small and the great are there,  
    and the slaves are free from their masters.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 20, '"Why is light given to one in misery,  
    and life to the bitter in soul,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 21, 'who long for death, but it does not come,  
    and dig for it more than for hidden treasures;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 22, 'who rejoice exceedingly,  
    and are glad when they find the grave?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 23, 'Why is light given to one who cannot see the way,  
    whom God has fenced in?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 24, 'For my sighing comes like my bread,  
    and my groanings are poured out like water.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 25, 'Truly the thing that I fear comes upon me,  
    and what I dread befalls me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 3, 26, 'I am not at ease, nor am I quiet;  
    I have no rest; but trouble comes."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 1, 'Then Eliphaz the Temanite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Eliphaz Speaks: Job Has Sinned'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 2, '"If one ventures a word with you, will you be offended?  
    But who can keep from speaking?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 3, 'See, you have instructed many;  
    you have strengthened the weak hands.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 4, 'Your words have supported those who were stumbling,  
    and you have made firm the feeble knees.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 5, 'But now it has come to you, and you are impatient;  
    it touches you, and you are dismayed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 6, 'Is not your fear of God your confidence,  
    and the integrity of your ways your hope?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 7, '"Think now, who that was innocent ever perished?  
    Or where were the upright cut off?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 8, 'As I have seen, those who plow iniquity  
    and sow trouble reap the same.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 9, 'By the breath of God they perish,  
    and by the blast of his anger they are consumed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 10, 'The roar of the lion, the voice of the fierce lion,  
    and the teeth of the young lions are broken.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 11, 'The strong lion perishes for lack of prey,  
    and the whelps of the lioness are scattered.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 12, '"Now a word came stealing to me,  
    my ear received the whisper of it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 13, 'Amid thoughts from visions of the night,  
    when deep sleep falls on mortals,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 14, 'dread came upon me, and trembling,  
    which made all my bones shake.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 15, 'A spirit glided past my face;  
    the hair of my flesh bristled.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 16, 'It stood still,  
    but I could not discern its appearance.  
A form was before my eyes;  
    there was silence, then I heard a voice:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 17, '"Can mortals be righteous before God?  
    Can human beings be pure before their Maker?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 18, 'Even in his servants he puts no trust,  
    and his angels he charges with error;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 19, 'how much more those who live in houses of clay,  
    whose foundation is in the dust,  
    who are crushed like a moth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 20, 'Between morning and evening they are destroyed;  
    they perish forever without any regarding it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 4, 21, 'Their tent-cord is plucked up within them,  
    and they die devoid of wisdom."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 1, '"Call now; is there anyone who will answer you?  
To which of the holy ones will you turn?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Is Corrected by God'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 2, 'Surely vexation kills the fool,  
    and jealousy slays the simple.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 3, 'I have seen fools taking root,  
    but suddenly I cursed their dwelling.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 4, 'Their children are far from safety,  
    they are crushed in the gate,  
    and there is no one to deliver them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 5, 'The hungry eat their harvest,  
    and they take it even out of the thorns;  
    and the thirsty pant after their wealth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 6, 'For misery does not come from the earth,  
    nor does trouble sprout from the ground;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 7, 'but human beings are born to trouble  
    just as sparks fly upward.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 8, '"As for me, I would seek God,  
    and to God I would commit my cause.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 9, 'He does great things and unsearchable,  
    marvelous things without number.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 10, 'He gives rain on the earth  
    and sends waters on the fields;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 11, 'he sets on high those who are lowly,  
    and those who mourn are lifted to safety.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 12, 'He frustrates the devices of the crafty,  
    so that their hands achieve no success.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 13, 'He takes the wise in their own craftiness;  
    and the schemes of the wily are brought to a quick end.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 14, 'They meet with darkness in the daytime,  
    and grope at noonday as in the night.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 15, 'But he saves the needy from the sword of their mouth,  
    from the hand of the mighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 16, 'So the poor have hope,  
    and injustice shuts its mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 17, '"How happy is the one whom God reproves;  
    therefore do not despise the discipline of the Almighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 18, 'For he wounds, but he binds up;  
    he strikes, but his hands heal.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 19, 'He will deliver you from six troubles;  
    in seven no harm shall touch you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 20, 'In famine he will redeem you from death,  
    and in war from the power of the sword.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 21, 'You shall be hidden from the scourge of the tongue,  
    and shall not fear destruction when it comes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 22, 'At destruction and famine you shall laugh,  
    and shall not fear the wild animals of the earth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 23, 'For you shall be in league with the stones of the field,  
    and the wild animals shall be at peace with you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 24, 'You shall know that your tent is safe,  
    you shall inspect your fold and miss nothing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 25, 'You shall know that your descendants will be many,  
    and your offspring like the grass of the earth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 26, 'You shall come to your grave in ripe old age,  
    as a shock of grain comes up to the threshing floor in its season.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 5, 27, 'See, we have searched this out; it is true.  
    Hear, and know it for yourself."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: My Complaint Is Just'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 2, '"O that my vexation were weighed,  
    and all my calamity laid in the balances!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 3, 'For then it would be heavier than the sand of the sea;  
    therefore my words have been rash.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 4, 'For the arrows of the Almighty are in me;  
    my spirit drinks their poison;  
    the terrors of God are arrayed against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 5, 'Does the wild ass bray over its grass,  
    or the ox low over its fodder?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 6, 'Can that which is tasteless be eaten without salt,  
    or is there any flavor in the juice of mallows?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 7, 'My appetite refuses to touch them;  
    they are like food that is loathsome to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 8, '"O that I might have my request,  
    and that God would grant my desire;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 9, 'that it would please God to crush me,  
    that he would let loose his hand and cut me off!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 10, 'This would be my consolation;  
    I would even exult in unrelenting pain;  
    for I have not denied the words of the Holy One.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 11, 'What is my strength, that I should wait?  
    And what is my end, that I should be patient?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 12, 'Is my strength the strength of stones,  
    or is my flesh bronze?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 13, 'In truth I have no help in me,  
    and any resource is driven from me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 14, '"Those who withhold kindness from a friend  
    forsake the fear of the Almighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 15, 'My companions are treacherous like a torrent-bed,  
    like freshets that pass away,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 16, 'that run dark with ice,  
    turbid with melting snow.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 17, 'In time of heat they disappear;  
    when it is hot, they vanish from their place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 18, 'The caravans turn aside from their course;  
    they go up into the waste, and perish.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 19, 'The caravans of Tema look,  
    the travelers of Sheba hope.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 20, 'They are disappointed because they were confident;  
    they come there and are confounded.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 21, 'Such you have now become to me;  
    you see my calamity, and are afraid.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 22, 'Have I said, "Make me a gift"?  
    Or, "From your wealth offer a bribe for me"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 23, 'Or, "Save me from an opponent"s hand"?  
    Or, "Ransom me from the hand of oppressors"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 24, '"Teach me, and I will be silent;  
    make me understand how I have gone wrong.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 25, 'How forceful are honest words!  
    But your reproof, what does it reprove?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 26, 'Do you think that you can reprove words,  
    as if the speech of the desperate were wind?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 27, 'You would even cast lots over the orphan,  
    and bargain over your friend.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 28, '"But now, be pleased to look at me;  
    for I will not lie to your face.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 29, 'Turn, I pray, let no wrong be done.  
    Turn now, my vindication is at stake.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 6, 30, 'Is there any wrong on my tongue?  
    Cannot my taste discern calamity?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 1, '"Do not human beings have a hard service on earth,  
    and are not their days like the days of a laborer?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job: My Suffering Is without End'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 2, 'Like a slave who longs for the shadow,  
    and like laborers who look for their wages,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 3, 'so I am allotted months of emptiness,  
    and nights of misery are apportioned to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 4, 'When I lie down I say, "When shall I rise?"  
    But the night is long,  
    and I am full of tossing until dawn.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 5, 'My flesh is clothed with worms and dirt;  
    my skin hardens, then breaks out again.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 6, 'My days are swifter than a weaver"s shuttle,  
    and come to their end without hope.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 7, '"Remember that my life is a breath;  
    my eye will never again see good.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 8, 'The eye that beholds me will see me no more;  
    while your eyes are upon me, I shall be gone.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 9, 'As the cloud fades and vanishes,  
    so those who go down to Sheol do not come up;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 10, 'they return no more to their houses,  
    nor do their places know them any more.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 11, '"Therefore I will not restrain my mouth;  
    I will speak in the anguish of my spirit;  
    I will complain in the bitterness of my soul.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 12, 'Am I the Sea, or the Dragon,  
    that you set a guard over me?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 13, 'When I say, "My bed will comfort me,  
    my couch will ease my complaint,"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 14, 'then you scare me with dreams  
    and terrify me with visions,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 15, 'so that I would choose strangling  
    and death rather than this body.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 16, 'I loathe my life; I would not live forever.  
    Let me alone, for my days are a breath.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 17, 'What are human beings, that you make so much of them,  
    that you set your mind on them,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 18, 'visit them every morning,  
    test them every moment?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 19, 'Will you not look away from me for a while,  
    let me alone until I swallow my spittle?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 20, 'If I sin, what do I do to you, you watcher of humanity?  
    Why have you made me your target?  
    Why have I become a burden to you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 7, 21, 'Why do you not pardon my transgression  
    and take away my iniquity?  
For now I shall lie in the earth;  
    you will seek me, but I shall not be."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 1, 'Then Bildad the Shuhite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Bildad Speaks: Job Should Repent'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 2, '"How long will you say these things,  
    and the words of your mouth be a great wind?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 3, 'Does God pervert justice?  
    Or does the Almighty pervert the right?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 4, 'If your children sinned against him,  
    he delivered them into the power of their transgression.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 5, 'If you will seek God  
    and make supplication to the Almighty,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 6, 'if you are pure and upright,  
    surely then he will rouse himself for you  
    and restore to you your rightful place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 7, 'Though your beginning was small,  
    your latter days will be very great.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 8, '"For inquire now of bygone generations,  
    and consider what their ancestors have found;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 9, 'for we are but of yesterday, and we know nothing,  
    for our days on earth are but a shadow.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 10, 'Will they not teach you and tell you  
    and utter words out of their understanding?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 11, '"Can papyrus grow where there is no marsh?  
    Can reeds flourish where there is no water?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 12, 'While yet in flower and not cut down,  
    they wither before any other plant.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 13, 'Such are the paths of all who forget God;  
    the hope of the godless shall perish.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 14, 'Their confidence is gossamer,  
    a spider"s house their trust.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 15, 'If one leans against its house, it will not stand;  
    if one lays hold of it, it will not endure.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 16, 'The wicked thrive before the sun,  
    and their shoots spread over the garden.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 17, 'Their roots twine around the stoneheap;  
    they live among the rocks.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 18, 'If they are destroyed from their place,  
    then it will deny them, saying, "I have never seen you."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 19, 'See, these are their happy ways,  
    and out of the earth still others will spring.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 20, '"See, God will not reject a blameless person,  
    nor take the hand of evildoers.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 21, 'He will yet fill your mouth with laughter,  
    and your lips with shouts of joy.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 8, 22, 'Those who hate you will be clothed with shame,  
    and the tent of the wicked will be no more."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: There Is No Mediator'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 2, '"Indeed I know that this is so;  
    but how can a mortal be just before God?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 3, 'If one wished to contend with him,  
    one could not answer him once in a thousand.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 4, 'He is wise in heart, and mighty in strength  
--who has resisted him, and succeeded?--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 5, 'he who removes mountains, and they do not know it,  
    when he overturns them in his anger;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 6, 'who shakes the earth out of its place,  
    and its pillars tremble;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 7, 'who commands the sun, and it does not rise;  
    who seals up the stars;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 8, 'who alone stretched out the heavens  
    and trampled the waves of the Sea;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 9, 'who made the Bear and Orion,  
    the Pleiades and the chambers of the south;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 10, 'who does great things beyond understanding,  
    and marvelous things without number.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 11, 'Look, he passes by me, and I do not see him;  
    he moves on, but I do not perceive him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 12, 'He snatches away; who can stop him?  
    Who will say to him, "What are you doing?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 13, '"God will not turn back his anger;  
    the helpers of Rahab bowed beneath him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 14, 'How then can I answer him,  
    choosing my words with him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 15, 'Though I am innocent, I cannot answer him;  
    I must appeal for mercy to my accuser.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 16, 'If I summoned him and he answered me,  
    I do not believe that he would listen to my voice.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 17, 'For he crushes me with a tempest,  
    and multiplies my wounds without cause;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 18, 'he will not let me get my breath,  
    but fills me with bitterness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 19, 'If it is a contest of strength, he is the strong one!  
    If it is a matter of justice, who can summon him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 20, 'Though I am innocent, my own mouth would condemn me;  
    though I am blameless, he would prove me perverse.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 21, 'I am blameless; I do not know myself;  
    I loathe my life.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 22, 'It is all one; therefore I say,  
    he destroys both the blameless and the wicked.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 23, 'When disaster brings sudden death,  
    he mocks at the calamity of the innocent.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 24, 'The earth is given into the hand of the wicked;  
    he covers the eyes of its judges--  
    if it is not he, who then is it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 25, '"My days are swifter than a runner;  
    they flee away, they see no good.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 26, 'They go by like skiffs of reed,  
    like an eagle swooping on the prey.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 27, 'If I say, "I will forget my complaint;  
    I will put off my sad countenance and be of good cheer,"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 28, 'I become afraid of all my suffering,  
    for I know you will not hold me innocent.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 29, 'I shall be condemned;  
    why then do I labor in vain?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 30, 'If I wash myself with soap  
    and cleanse my hands with lye,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 31, 'yet you will plunge me into filth,  
    and my own clothes will abhor me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 32, 'For he is not a mortal, as I am, that I might answer him,  
    that we should come to trial together.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 33, 'There is no umpire between us,  
    who might lay his hand on us both.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 34, 'If he would take his rod away from me,  
    and not let dread of him terrify me,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 9, 35, 'then I would speak without fear of him,  
    for I know I am not what I am thought to be.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 1, '"I loathe my life;  
    I will give free utterance to my complaint;  
    I will speak in the bitterness of my soul.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job: I Loathe My Life'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 2, 'I will say to God, Do not condemn me;  
    let me know why you contend against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 3, 'Does it seem good to you to oppress,  
    to despise the work of your hands  
    and favor the schemes of the wicked?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 4, 'Do you have eyes of flesh?  
    Do you see as humans see?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 5, 'Are your days like the days of mortals,  
    or your years like human years,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 6, 'that you seek out my iniquity  
    and search for my sin,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 7, 'although you know that I am not guilty,  
    and there is no one to deliver out of your hand?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 8, 'Your hands fashioned and made me;  
    and now you turn and destroy me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 9, 'Remember that you fashioned me like clay;  
    and will you turn me to dust again?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 10, 'Did you not pour me out like milk  
    and curdle me like cheese?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 11, 'You clothed me with skin and flesh,  
    and knit me together with bones and sinews.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 12, 'You have granted me life and steadfast love,  
    and your care has preserved my spirit.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 13, 'Yet these things you hid in your heart;  
    I know that this was your purpose.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 14, 'If I sin, you watch me,  
    and do not acquit me of my iniquity.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 15, 'If I am wicked, woe to me!  
    If I am righteous, I cannot lift up my head,  
for I am filled with disgrace  
    and look upon my affliction.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 16, 'Bold as a lion you hunt me;  
    you repeat your exploits against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 17, 'You renew your witnesses against me,  
    and increase your vexation toward me;  
    you bring fresh troops against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 18, '"Why did you bring me forth from the womb?  
    Would that I had died before any eye had seen me,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 19, 'and were as though I had not been,  
    carried from the womb to the grave.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 20, 'Are not the days of my life few?  
    Let me alone, that I may find a little comfort', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 21, 'before I go, never to return,  
    to the land of gloom and deep darkness,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 10, 22, 'the land of gloom and chaos,  
    where light is like darkness."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 1, 'Then Zophar the Naamathite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Zophar Speaks: Job"s Guilt Deserves Punishment'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 2, '"Should a multitude of words go unanswered,  
    and should one full of talk be vindicated?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 3, 'Should your babble put others to silence,  
    and when you mock, shall no one shame you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 4, 'For you say, "My conduct is pure,  
    and I am clean in God"s sight."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 5, 'But O that God would speak,  
    and open his lips to you,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 6, 'and that he would tell you the secrets of wisdom!  
    For wisdom is many-sided.  
Know then that God exacts of you less than your guilt deserves.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 7, '"Can you find out the deep things of God?  
    Can you find out the limit of the Almighty?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 8, 'It is higher than heaven--what can you do?  
    Deeper than Sheol--what can you know?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 9, 'Its measure is longer than the earth,  
    and broader than the sea.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 10, 'If he passes through, and imprisons,  
    and assembles for judgment, who can hinder him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 11, 'For he knows those who are worthless;  
    when he sees iniquity, will he not consider it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 12, 'But a stupid person will get understanding,  
    when a wild ass is born human.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 13, '"If you direct your heart rightly,  
    you will stretch out your hands toward him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 14, 'If iniquity is in your hand, put it far away,  
    and do not let wickedness reside in your tents.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 15, 'Surely then you will lift up your face without blemish;  
    you will be secure, and will not fear.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 16, 'You will forget your misery;  
    you will remember it as waters that have passed away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 17, 'And your life will be brighter than the noonday;  
    its darkness will be like the morning.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 18, 'And you will have confidence, because there is hope;  
    you will be protected and take your rest in safety.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 19, 'You will lie down, and no one will make you afraid;  
    many will entreat your favor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 11, 20, 'But the eyes of the wicked will fail;  
    all way of escape will be lost to them,  
    and their hope is to breathe their last."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: I Am a Laughingstock'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 2, '"No doubt you are the people,  
    and wisdom will die with you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 3, 'But I have understanding as well as you;  
    I am not inferior to you.  
    Who does not know such things as these?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 4, 'I am a laughingstock to my friends;  
    I, who called upon God and he answered me,  
    a just and blameless man, I am a laughingstock.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 5, 'Those at ease have contempt for misfortune,  
    but it is ready for those whose feet are unstable.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 6, 'The tents of robbers are at peace,  
    and those who provoke God are secure,  
    who bring their god in their hands.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 7, '"But ask the animals, and they will teach you;  
    the birds of the air, and they will tell you;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 8, 'ask the plants of the earth, and they will teach you;  
    and the fish of the sea will declare to you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 9, 'Who among all these does not know  
    that the hand of the LORD has done this?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 10, 'In his hand is the life of every living thing  
    and the breath of every human being.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 11, 'Does not the ear test words  
    as the palate tastes food?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 12, 'Is wisdom with the aged,  
    and understanding in length of days?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 13, '"With God are wisdom and strength;  
    he has counsel and understanding.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 14, 'If he tears down, no one can rebuild;  
    if he shuts someone in, no one can open up.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 15, 'If he withholds the waters, they dry up;  
    if he sends them out, they overwhelm the land.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 16, 'With him are strength and wisdom;  
    the deceived and the deceiver are his.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 17, 'He leads counselors away stripped,  
    and makes fools of judges.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 18, 'He looses the sash of kings,  
    and binds a waistcloth on their loins.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 19, 'He leads priests away stripped,  
    and overthrows the mighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 20, 'He deprives of speech those who are trusted,  
    and takes away the discernment of the elders.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 21, 'He pours contempt on princes,  
    and looses the belt of the strong.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 22, 'He uncovers the deeps out of darkness,  
    and brings deep darkness to light.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 23, 'He makes nations great, then destroys them;  
    he enlarges nations, then leads them away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 24, 'He strips understanding from the leaders of the earth,  
    and makes them wander in a pathless waste.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 12, 25, 'They grope in the dark without light;  
    he makes them stagger like a drunkard.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 1, '"Look, my eye has seen all this,  
    my ear has heard and understood it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 2, 'What you know, I also know;  
    I am not inferior to you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 3, 'But I would speak to the Almighty,  
    and I desire to argue my case with God.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 4, 'As for you, you whitewash with lies;  
    all of you are worthless physicians.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 5, 'If you would only keep silent,  
    that would be your wisdom!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 6, 'Hear now my reasoning,  
    and listen to the pleadings of my lips.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 7, 'Will you speak falsely for God,  
    and speak deceitfully for him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 8, 'Will you show partiality toward him,  
    will you plead the case for God?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 9, 'Will it be well with you when he searches you out?  
    Or can you deceive him, as one person deceives another?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 10, 'He will surely rebuke you  
    if in secret you show partiality.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 11, 'Will not his majesty terrify you,  
    and the dread of him fall upon you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 12, 'Your maxims are proverbs of ashes,  
    your defenses are defenses of clay.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 13, '"Let me have silence, and I will speak,  
    and let come on me what may.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 14, 'I will take my flesh in my teeth,  
    and put my life in my hand.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 15, 'See, he will kill me; I have no hope;  
    but I will defend my ways to his face.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 16, 'This will be my salvation,  
    that the godless shall not come before him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 17, 'Listen carefully to my words,  
    and let my declaration be in your ears.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 18, 'I have indeed prepared my case;  
    I know that I shall be vindicated.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 19, 'Who is there that will contend with me?  
    For then I would be silent and die.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 20, 'Only grant two things to me,  
    then I will not hide myself from your face:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job"s Despondent Prayer'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 21, 'withdraw your hand far from me,  
    and do not let dread of you terrify me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 22, 'Then call, and I will answer;  
    or let me speak, and you reply to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 23, 'How many are my iniquities and my sins?  
    Make me know my transgression and my sin.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 24, 'Why do you hide your face,  
    and count me as your enemy?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 25, 'Will you frighten a windblown leaf  
    and pursue dry chaff?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 26, 'For you write bitter things against me,  
    and make me reap the iniquities of my youth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 27, 'You put my feet in the stocks,  
    and watch all my paths;  
    you set a bound to the soles of my feet.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 13, 28, 'One wastes away like a rotten thing,  
    like a garment that is moth-eaten.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 1, '"A mortal, born of woman, few of days and full of trouble,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 2, 'comes up like a flower and withers,  
    flees like a shadow and does not last.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 3, 'Do you fix your eyes on such a one?  
    Do you bring me into judgment with you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 4, 'Who can bring a clean thing out of an unclean?  
    No one can.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 5, 'Since their days are determined,  
    and the number of their months is known to you,  
    and you have appointed the bounds that they cannot pass,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 6, 'look away from them, and desist,  
    that they may enjoy, like laborers, their days.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 7, '"For there is hope for a tree,  
    if it is cut down, that it will sprout again,  
    and that its shoots will not cease.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 8, 'Though its root grows old in the earth,  
    and its stump dies in the ground,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 9, 'yet at the scent of water it will bud  
    and put forth branches like a young plant.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 10, 'But mortals die, and are laid low;  
    humans expire, and where are they?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 11, 'As waters fail from a lake,  
    and a river wastes away and dries up,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 12, 'so mortals lie down and do not rise again;  
    until the heavens are no more, they will not awake  
    or be roused out of their sleep.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 13, 'O that you would hide me in Sheol,  
    that you would conceal me until your wrath is past,  
    that you would appoint me a set time, and remember me!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 14, 'If mortals die, will they live again?  
    All the days of my service I would wait  
    until my release should come.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 15, 'You would call, and I would answer you;  
    you would long for the work of your hands.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 16, 'For then you would not number my steps,  
    you would not keep watch over my sin;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 17, 'my transgression would be sealed up in a bag,  
    and you would cover over my iniquity.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 18, '"But the mountain falls and crumbles away,  
    and the rock is removed from its place;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 19, 'the waters wear away the stones;  
    the torrents wash away the soil of the earth;  
    so you destroy the hope of mortals.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 20, 'You prevail forever against them, and they pass away;  
    you change their countenance, and send them away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 21, 'Their children come to honor, and they do not know it;  
    they are brought low, and it goes unnoticed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 14, 22, 'They feel only the pain of their own bodies,  
    and mourn only for themselves."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 1, 'Then Eliphaz the Temanite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Eliphaz Speaks: Job Undermines Religion'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 2, '"Should the wise answer with windy knowledge,  
    and fill themselves with the east wind?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 3, 'Should they argue in unprofitable talk,  
    or in words with which they can do no good?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 4, 'But you are doing away with the fear of God,  
    and hindering meditation before God.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 5, 'For your iniquity teaches your mouth,  
    and you choose the tongue of the crafty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 6, 'Your own mouth condemns you, and not I;  
    your own lips testify against you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 7, '"Are you the firstborn of the human race?  
    Were you brought forth before the hills?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 8, 'Have you listened in the council of God?  
    And do you limit wisdom to yourself?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 9, 'What do you know that we do not know?  
    What do you understand that is not clear to us?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 10, 'The gray-haired and the aged are on our side,  
    those older than your father.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 11, 'Are the consolations of God too small for you,  
    or the word that deals gently with you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 12, 'Why does your heart carry you away,  
    and why do your eyes flash,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 13, 'so that you turn your spirit against God,  
    and let such words go out of your mouth?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 14, 'What are mortals, that they can be clean?  
    Or those born of woman, that they can be righteous?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 15, 'God puts no trust even in his holy ones,  
    and the heavens are not clean in his sight;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 16, 'how much less one who is abominable and corrupt,  
    one who drinks iniquity like water!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 17, '"I will show you; listen to me;  
    what I have seen I will declare--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 18, 'what sages have told,  
    and their ancestors have not hidden,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 19, 'to whom alone the land was given,  
    and no stranger passed among them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 20, 'The wicked writhe in pain all their days,  
    through all the years that are laid up for the ruthless.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 21, 'Terrifying sounds are in their ears;  
    in prosperity the destroyer will come upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 22, 'They despair of returning from darkness,  
    and they are destined for the sword.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 23, 'They wander abroad for bread, saying, "Where is it?"  
    They know that a day of darkness is ready at hand;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 24, 'distress and anguish terrify them;  
    they prevail against them, like a king prepared for battle.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 25, 'Because they stretched out their hands against God,  
    and bid defiance to the Almighty,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 26, 'running stubbornly against him  
    with a thick-bossed shield;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 27, 'because they have covered their faces with their fat,  
    and gathered fat upon their loins,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 28, 'they will live in desolate cities,  
    in houses that no one should inhabit,  
    houses destined to become heaps of ruins;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 29, 'they will not be rich, and their wealth will not endure,  
    nor will they strike root in the earth;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 30, 'they will not escape from darkness;  
    the flame will dry up their shoots,  
    and their blossom will be swept away by the wind.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 31, 'Let them not trust in emptiness, deceiving themselves;  
    for emptiness will be their recompense.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 32, 'It will be paid in full before their time,  
    and their branch will not be green.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 33, 'They will shake off their unripe grape, like the vine,  
    and cast off their blossoms, like the olive tree.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 34, 'For the company of the godless is barren,  
    and fire consumes the tents of bribery.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 15, 35, 'They conceive mischief and bring forth evil  
    and their heart prepares deceit."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Reaffirms His Innocence'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 2, '"I have heard many such things;  
    miserable comforters are you all.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 3, 'Have windy words no limit?  
    Or what provokes you that you keep on talking?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 4, 'I also could talk as you do,  
    if you were in my place;  
I could join words together against you,  
    and shake my head at you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 5, 'I could encourage you with my mouth,  
    and the solace of my lips would assuage your pain.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 6, '"If I speak, my pain is not assuaged,  
    and if I forbear, how much of it leaves me?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 7, 'Surely now God has worn me out;  
    he has made desolate all my company.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 8, 'And he has shriveled me up,  
    which is a witness against me;  
my leanness has risen up against me,  
    and it testifies to my face.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 9, 'He has torn me in his wrath, and hated me;  
    he has gnashed his teeth at me;  
    my adversary sharpens his eyes against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 10, 'They have gaped at me with their mouths;  
    they have struck me insolently on the cheek;  
    they mass themselves together against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 11, 'God gives me up to the ungodly,  
    and casts me into the hands of the wicked.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 12, 'I was at ease, and he broke me in two;  
    he seized me by the neck and dashed me to pieces;  
he set me up as his target;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 13, 'his archers surround me.  
He slashes open my kidneys, and shows no mercy;  
    he pours out my gall on the ground.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 14, 'He bursts upon me again and again;  
    he rushes at me like a warrior.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 15, 'I have sewed sackcloth upon my skin,  
    and have laid my strength in the dust.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 16, 'My face is red with weeping,  
    and deep darkness is on my eyelids,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 17, 'though there is no violence in my hands,  
    and my prayer is pure.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 18, '"O earth, do not cover my blood;  
    let my outcry find no resting place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 19, 'Even now, in fact, my witness is in heaven,  
    and he that vouches for me is on high.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 20, 'My friends scorn me;  
    my eye pours out tears to God,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 21, 'that he would maintain the right of a mortal with God,  
    as one does for a neighbor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 16, 22, 'For when a few years have come,  
    I shall go the way from which I shall not return.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 1, 'My spirit is broken, my days are extinct,  
    the grave is ready for me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Prays for Relief'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 2, 'Surely there are mockers around me,  
    and my eye dwells on their provocation.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 3, '"Lay down a pledge for me with yourself;  
    who is there that will give surety for me?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 4, 'Since you have closed their minds to understanding,  
    therefore you will not let them triumph.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 5, 'Those who denounce friends for reward--  
    the eyes of their children will fail.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 6, '"He has made me a byword of the peoples,  
    and I am one before whom people spit.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 7, 'My eye has grown dim from grief,  
    and all my members are like a shadow.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 8, 'The upright are appalled at this,  
    and the innocent stir themselves up against the godless.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 9, 'Yet the righteous hold to their way,  
    and they that have clean hands grow stronger and stronger.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 10, 'But you, come back now, all of you,  
    and I shall not find a sensible person among you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 11, 'My days are past, my plans are broken off,  
    the desires of my heart.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 12, 'They make night into day;  
    "The light," they say, "is near to the darkness."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 13, 'If I look for Sheol as my house,  
    if I spread my couch in darkness,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 14, 'if I say to the Pit, "You are my father,"  
    and to the worm, "My mother," or "My sister,"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 15, 'where then is my hope?  
    Who will see my hope?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 17, 16, 'Will it go down to the bars of Sheol?  
    Shall we descend together into the dust?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 1, 'Then Bildad the Shuhite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Bildad Speaks: God Punishes the Wicked'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 2, '"How long will you hunt for words?  
    Consider, and then we shall speak.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 3, 'Why are we counted as cattle?  
    Why are we stupid in your sight?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 4, 'You who tear yourself in your anger--  
    shall the earth be forsaken because of you,  
    or the rock be removed out of its place?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 5, '"Surely the light of the wicked is put out,  
    and the flame of their fire does not shine.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 6, 'The light is dark in their tent,  
    and the lamp above them is put out.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 7, 'Their strong steps are shortened,  
    and their own schemes throw them down.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 8, 'For they are thrust into a net by their own feet,  
    and they walk into a pitfall.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 9, 'A trap seizes them by the heel;  
    a snare lays hold of them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 10, 'A rope is hid for them in the ground,  
    a trap for them in the path.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 11, 'Terrors frighten them on every side,  
    and chase them at their heels.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 12, 'Their strength is consumed by hunger,  
    and calamity is ready for their stumbling.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 13, 'By disease their skin is consumed,  
    the firstborn of Death consumes their limbs.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 14, 'They are torn from the tent in which they trusted,  
    and are brought to the king of terrors.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 15, 'In their tents nothing remains;  
    sulfur is scattered upon their habitations.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 16, 'Their roots dry up beneath,  
    and their branches wither above.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 17, 'Their memory perishes from the earth,  
    and they have no name in the street.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 18, 'They are thrust from light into darkness,  
    and driven out of the world.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 19, 'They have no offspring or descendant among their people,  
    and no survivor where they used to live.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 20, 'They of the west are appalled at their fate,  
    and horror seizes those of the east.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 18, 21, 'Surely such are the dwellings of the ungodly,  
    such is the place of those who do not know God."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: I Know That My Redeemer Lives'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 2, '"How long will you torment me,  
    and break me in pieces with words?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 3, 'These ten times you have cast reproach upon me;  
    are you not ashamed to wrong me?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 4, 'And even if it is true that I have erred,  
    my error remains with me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 5, 'If indeed you magnify yourselves against me,  
    and make my humiliation an argument against me,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 6, 'know then that God has put me in the wrong,  
    and closed his net around me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 7, 'Even when I cry out, "Violence!" I am not answered;  
    I call aloud, but there is no justice.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 8, 'He has walled up my way so that I cannot pass,  
    and he has set darkness upon my paths.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 9, 'He has stripped my glory from me,  
    and taken the crown from my head.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 10, 'He breaks me down on every side, and I am gone,  
    he has uprooted my hope like a tree.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 11, 'He has kindled his wrath against me,  
    and counts me as his adversary.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 12, 'His troops come on together;  
    they have thrown up siegeworks against me,  
    and encamp around my tent.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 13, '"He has put my family far from me,  
    and my acquaintances are wholly estranged from me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 14, 'My relatives and my close friends have failed me;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 15, 'the guests in my house have forgotten me;  
my serving girls count me as a stranger;  
    I have become an alien in their eyes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 16, 'I call to my servant, but he gives me no answer;  
    I must myself plead with him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 17, 'My breath is repulsive to my wife;  
    I am loathsome to my own family.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 18, 'Even young children despise me;  
    when I rise, they talk against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 19, 'All my intimate friends abhor me,  
    and those whom I loved have turned against me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 20, 'My bones cling to my skin and to my flesh,  
    and I have escaped by the skin of my teeth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 21, 'Have pity on me, have pity on me, O you my friends,  
    for the hand of God has touched me!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 22, 'Why do you, like God, pursue me,  
    never satisfied with my flesh?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 23, '"O that my words were written down!  
    O that they were inscribed in a book!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 24, 'O that with an iron pen and with lead  
    they were engraved on a rock forever!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 25, 'For I know that my Redeemer lives,  
    and that at the last he will stand upon the earth;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 26, 'and after my skin has been thus destroyed,  
    then in my flesh I shall see God,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 27, 'whom I shall see on my side,  
    and my eyes shall behold, and not another.  
    My heart faints within me!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 28, 'If you say, "How we will persecute him!"  
    and, "The root of the matter is found in him";', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 19, 29, 'be afraid of the sword,  
    for wrath brings the punishment of the sword,  
    so that you may know there is a judgment."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 1, 'Then Zophar the Naamathite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Zophar Speaks: Wickedness Receives Just Retribution'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 2, '"Pay attention! My thoughts urge me to answer,  
    because of the agitation within me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 3, 'I hear censure that insults me,  
    and a spirit beyond my understanding answers me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 4, 'Do you not know this from of old,  
    ever since mortals were placed on earth,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 5, 'that the exulting of the wicked is short,  
    and the joy of the godless is but for a moment?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 6, 'Even though they mount up high as the heavens,  
    and their head reaches to the clouds,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 7, 'they will perish forever like their own dung;  
    those who have seen them will say, "Where are they?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 8, 'They will fly away like a dream, and not be found;  
    they will be chased away like a vision of the night.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 9, 'The eye that saw them will see them no more,  
    nor will their place behold them any longer.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 10, 'Their children will seek the favor of the poor,  
    and their hands will give back their wealth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 11, 'Their bodies, once full of youth,  
    will lie down in the dust with them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 12, '"Though wickedness is sweet in their mouth,  
    though they hide it under their tongues,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 13, 'though they are loath to let it go,  
    and hold it in their mouths,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 14, 'yet their food is turned in their stomachs;  
    it is the venom of asps within them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 15, 'They swallow down riches and vomit them up again;  
    God casts them out of their bellies.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 16, 'They will suck the poison of asps;  
    the tongue of a viper will kill them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 17, 'They will not look on the rivers,  
    the streams flowing with honey and curds.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 18, 'They will give back the fruit of their toil,  
    and will not swallow it down;  
from the profit of their trading  
    they will get no enjoyment.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 19, 'For they have crushed and abandoned the poor,  
    they have seized a house that they did not build.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 20, '"They knew no quiet in their bellies;  
    in their greed they let nothing escape.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 21, 'There was nothing left after they had eaten;  
    therefore their prosperity will not endure.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 22, 'In full sufficiency they will be in distress;  
    all the force of misery will come upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 23, 'To fill their belly to the full  
    God will send his fierce anger into them,  
    and rain it upon them as their food.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 24, 'They will flee from an iron weapon;  
    a bronze arrow will strike them through.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 25, 'It is drawn forth and comes out of their body,  
    and the glittering point comes out of their gall;  
    terrors come upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 26, 'Utter darkness is laid up for their treasures;  
    a fire fanned by no one will devour them;  
    what is left in their tent will be consumed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 27, 'The heavens will reveal their iniquity,  
    and the earth will rise up against them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 28, 'The possessions of their house will be carried away,  
    dragged off in the day of God"s wrath.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 20, 29, 'This is the portion of the wicked from God,  
    the heritage decreed for them by God."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: The Wicked Often Go Unpunished'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 2, '"Listen carefully to my words,  
    and let this be your consolation.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 3, 'Bear with me, and I will speak;  
    then after I have spoken, mock on.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 4, 'As for me, is my complaint addressed to mortals?  
    Why should I not be impatient?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 5, 'Look at me, and be appalled,  
    and lay your hand upon your mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 6, 'When I think of it I am dismayed,  
    and shuddering seizes my flesh.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 7, 'Why do the wicked live on,  
    reach old age, and grow mighty in power?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 8, 'Their children are established in their presence,  
    and their offspring before their eyes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 9, 'Their houses are safe from fear,  
    and no rod of God is upon them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 10, 'Their bull breeds without fail;  
    their cow calves and never miscarries.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 11, 'They send out their little ones like a flock,  
    and their children dance around.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 12, 'They sing to the tambourine and the lyre,  
    and rejoice to the sound of the pipe.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 13, 'They spend their days in prosperity,  
    and in peace they go down to Sheol.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 14, 'They say to God, "Leave us alone!  
    We do not desire to know your ways.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 15, 'What is the Almighty, that we should serve him?  
    And what profit do we get if we pray to him?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 16, 'Is not their prosperity indeed their own achievement?  
    The plans of the wicked are repugnant to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 17, '"How often is the lamp of the wicked put out?  
    How often does calamity come upon them?  
    How often does God distribute pains in his anger?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 18, 'How often are they like straw before the wind,  
    and like chaff that the storm carries away?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 19, 'You say, "God stores up their iniquity for their children."  
    Let it be paid back to them, so that they may know it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 20, 'Let their own eyes see their destruction,  
    and let them drink of the wrath of the Almighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 21, 'For what do they care for their household after them,  
    when the number of their months is cut off?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 22, 'Will any teach God knowledge,  
    seeing that he judges those that are on high?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 23, 'One dies in full prosperity,  
    being wholly at ease and secure,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 24, 'his loins full of milk  
    and the marrow of his bones moist.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 25, 'Another dies in bitterness of soul,  
    never having tasted of good.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 26, 'They lie down alike in the dust,  
    and the worms cover them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 27, '"Oh, I know your thoughts,  
    and your schemes to wrong me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 28, 'For you say, "Where is the house of the prince?  
    Where is the tent in which the wicked lived?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 29, 'Have you not asked those who travel the roads,  
    and do you not accept their testimony,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 30, 'that the wicked are spared in the day of calamity,  
    and are rescued in the day of wrath?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 31, 'Who declares their way to their face,  
    and who repays them for what they have done?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 32, 'When they are carried to the grave,  
    a watch is kept over their tomb.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 33, 'The clods of the valley are sweet to them;  
    everyone will follow after,  
    and those who went before are innumerable.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 21, 34, 'How then will you comfort me with empty nothings?  
    There is nothing left of your answers but falsehood."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 1, 'Then Eliphaz the Temanite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Eliphaz Speaks: Job"s Wickedness Is Great'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 2, '"Can a mortal be of use to God?  
    Can even the wisest be of service to him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 3, 'Is it any pleasure to the Almighty if you are righteous,  
    or is it gain to him if you make your ways blameless?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 4, 'Is it for your piety that he reproves you,  
    and enters into judgment with you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 5, 'Is not your wickedness great?  
    There is no end to your iniquities.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 6, 'For you have exacted pledges from your family for no reason,  
    and stripped the naked of their clothing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 7, 'You have given no water to the weary to drink,  
    and you have withheld bread from the hungry.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 8, 'The powerful possess the land,  
    and the favored live in it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 9, 'You have sent widows away empty-handed,  
    and the arms of the orphans you have crushed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 10, 'Therefore snares are around you,  
    and sudden terror overwhelms you,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 11, 'or darkness so that you cannot see;  
    a flood of water covers you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 12, '"Is not God high in the heavens?  
    See the highest stars, how lofty they are!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 13, 'Therefore you say, "What does God know?  
    Can he judge through the deep darkness?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 14, 'Thick clouds enwrap him, so that he does not see,  
    and he walks on the dome of heaven."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 15, 'Will you keep to the old way  
    that the wicked have trod?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 16, 'They were snatched away before their time;  
    their foundation was washed away by a flood.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 17, 'They said to God, "Leave us alone,"  
    and "What can the Almighty do to us?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 18, 'Yet he filled their houses with good things--  
    but the plans of the wicked are repugnant to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 19, 'The righteous see it and are glad;  
    the innocent laugh them to scorn,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 20, 'saying, "Surely our adversaries are cut off,  
    and what they left, the fire has consumed."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 21, '"Agree with God, and be at peace;  
    in this way good will come to you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 22, 'Receive instruction from his mouth,  
    and lay up his words in your heart.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 23, 'If you return to the Almighty, you will be restored,  
    if you remove unrighteousness from your tents,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 24, 'if you treat gold like dust,  
    and gold of Ophir like the stones of the torrent-bed,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 25, 'and if the Almighty is your gold  
    and your precious silver,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 26, 'then you will delight yourself in the Almighty,  
    and lift up your face to God.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 27, 'You will pray to him, and he will hear you,  
    and you will pay your vows.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 28, 'You will decide on a matter, and it will be established for you,  
    and light will shine on your ways.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 29, 'When others are humiliated, you say it is pride;  
    for he saves the humble.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 22, 30, 'He will deliver even those who are guilty;  
    they will escape because of the cleanness of your hands."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: My Complaint Is Bitter'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 2, '"Today also my complaint is bitter;  
    his hand is heavy despite my groaning.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 3, 'Oh, that I knew where I might find him,  
    that I might come even to his dwelling!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 4, 'I would lay my case before him,  
    and fill my mouth with arguments.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 5, 'I would learn what he would answer me,  
    and understand what he would say to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 6, 'Would he contend with me in the greatness of his power?  
    No; but he would give heed to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 7, 'There an upright person could reason with him,  
    and I should be acquitted forever by my judge.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 8, '"If I go forward, he is not there;  
    or backward, I cannot perceive him;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 9, 'on the left he hides, and I cannot behold him;  
    I turn to the right, but I cannot see him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 10, 'But he knows the way that I take;  
    when he has tested me, I shall come out like gold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 11, 'My foot has held fast to his steps;  
    I have kept his way and have not turned aside.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 12, 'I have not departed from the commandment of his lips;  
    I have treasured in my bosom the words of his mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 13, 'But he stands alone and who can dissuade him?  
    What he desires, that he does.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 14, 'For he will complete what he appoints for me;  
    and many such things are in his mind.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 15, 'Therefore I am terrified at his presence;  
    when I consider, I am in dread of him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 16, 'God has made my heart faint;  
    the Almighty has terrified me;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 23, 17, 'If only I could vanish in darkness,  
    and thick darkness would cover my face!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 1, '"Why are times not kept by the Almighty,  
    and why do those who know him never see his days?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Complains of Violence on the Earth'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 2, 'The wicked remove landmarks;  
    they seize flocks and pasture them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 3, 'They drive away the donkey of the orphan;  
    they take the widow"s ox for a pledge.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 4, 'They thrust the needy off the road;  
    the poor of the earth all hide themselves.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 5, 'Like wild asses in the desert  
    they go out to their toil,  
scavenging in the wasteland  
    food for their young.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 6, 'They reap in a field not their own  
    and they glean in the vineyard of the wicked.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 7, 'They lie all night naked, without clothing,  
    and have no covering in the cold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 8, 'They are wet with the rain of the mountains,  
    and cling to the rock for want of shelter.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 9, '"There are those who snatch the orphan child from the breast,  
    and take as a pledge the infant of the poor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 10, 'They go about naked, without clothing;  
    though hungry, they carry the sheaves;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 11, 'between their terraces they press out oil;  
    they tread the wine presses, but suffer thirst.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 12, 'From the city the dying groan,  
    and the throat of the wounded cries for help;  
    yet God pays no attention to their prayer.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 13, '"There are those who rebel against the light,  
    who are not acquainted with its ways,  
    and do not stay in its paths.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 14, 'The murderer rises at dusk  
    to kill the poor and needy,  
    and in the night is like a thief.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 15, 'The eye of the adulterer also waits for the twilight,  
    saying, "No eye will see me";  
    and he disguises his face.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 16, 'In the dark they dig through houses;  
    by day they shut themselves up;  
    they do not know the light.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 17, 'For deep darkness is morning to all of them;  
    for they are friends with the terrors of deep darkness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 18, '"Swift are they on the face of the waters;  
    their portion in the land is cursed;  
    no treader turns toward their vineyards.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 19, 'Drought and heat snatch away the snow waters;  
    so does Sheol those who have sinned.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 20, 'The womb forgets them;  
    the worm finds them sweet;  
they are no longer remembered;  
    so wickedness is broken like a tree.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 21, '"They harm the childless woman,  
    and do no good to the widow.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 22, 'Yet God prolongs the life of the mighty by his power;  
    they rise up when they despair of life.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 23, 'He gives them security, and they are supported;  
    his eyes are upon their ways.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 24, 'They are exalted a little while, and then are gone;  
    they wither and fade like the mallow;  
    they are cut off like the heads of grain.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 24, 25, 'If it is not so, who will prove me a liar,  
    and show that there is nothing in what I say?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 1, 'Then Bildad the Shuhite answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Bildad Speaks: How Can a Mortal Be Righteous Before God?'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 2, '"Dominion and fear are with God;  
    he makes peace in his high heaven.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 3, 'Is there any number to his armies?  
    Upon whom does his light not arise?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 4, 'How then can a mortal be righteous before God?  
    How can one born of woman be pure?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 5, 'If even the moon is not bright  
    and the stars are not pure in his sight,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 25, 6, 'how much less a mortal, who is a maggot,  
    and a human being, who is a worm!"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 1, 'Then Job answered:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Replies: God"s Majesty Is Unsearchable'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 2, '"How you have helped one who has no power!  
    How you have assisted the arm that has no strength!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 3, 'How you have counseled one who has no wisdom,  
    and given much good advice!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 4, 'With whose help have you uttered words,  
    and whose spirit has come forth from you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 5, 'The shades below tremble,  
    the waters and their inhabitants.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 6, 'Sheol is naked before God,  
    and Abaddon has no covering.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 7, 'He stretches out Zaphon over the void,  
    and hangs the earth upon nothing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 8, 'He binds up the waters in his thick clouds,  
    and the cloud is not torn open by them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 9, 'He covers the face of the full moon,  
    and spreads over it his cloud.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 10, 'He has described a circle on the face of the waters,  
    at the boundary between light and darkness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 11, 'The pillars of heaven tremble,  
    and are astounded at his rebuke.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 12, 'By his power he stilled the Sea;  
    by his understanding he struck down Rahab.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 13, 'By his wind the heavens were made fair;  
    his hand pierced the fleeing serpent.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 26, 14, 'These are indeed but the outskirts of his ways;  
    and how small a whisper do we hear of him!  
    But the thunder of his power who can understand?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 1, 'Job again took up his discourse and said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Maintains His Integrity'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 2, '"As God lives, who has taken away my right,  
    and the Almighty, who has made my soul bitter,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 3, 'as long as my breath is in me  
    and the spirit of God is in my nostrils,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 4, 'my lips will not speak falsehood,  
    and my tongue will not utter deceit.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 5, 'Far be it from me to say that you are right;  
    until I die I will not put away my integrity from me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 6, 'I hold fast my righteousness, and will not let it go;  
    my heart does not reproach me for any of my days.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 7, '"May my enemy be like the wicked,  
    and may my opponent be like the unrighteous.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 8, 'For what is the hope of the godless when God cuts them off,  
    when God takes away their lives?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 9, 'Will God hear their cry  
    when trouble comes upon them?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 10, 'Will they take delight in the Almighty?  
    Will they call upon God at all times?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 11, 'I will teach you concerning the hand of God;  
    that which is with the Almighty I will not conceal.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 12, 'All of you have seen it yourselves;  
    why then have you become altogether vain?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 13, '"This is the portion of the wicked with God,  
    and the heritage that oppressors receive from the Almighty:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 14, 'If their children are multiplied, it is for the sword;  
    and their offspring have not enough to eat.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 15, 'Those who survive them the pestilence buries,  
    and their widows make no lamentation.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 16, 'Though they heap up silver like dust,  
    and pile up clothing like clay--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 17, 'they may pile it up, but the just will wear it,  
    and the innocent will divide the silver.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 18, 'They build their houses like nests,  
    like booths made by sentinels of the vineyard.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 19, 'They go to bed with wealth, but will do so no more;  
    they open their eyes, and it is gone.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 20, 'Terrors overtake them like a flood;  
    in the night a whirlwind carries them off.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 21, 'The east wind lifts them up and they are gone;  
    it sweeps them out of their place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 22, 'It hurls at them without pity;  
    they flee from its power in headlong flight.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 27, 23, 'It claps its hands at them,  
    and hisses at them from its place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 1, '"Surely there is a mine for silver,  
    and a place for gold to be refined.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Interlude: Where Wisdom Is Found'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 2, 'Iron is taken out of the earth,  
    and copper is smelted from ore.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 3, 'Miners put an end to darkness,  
    and search out to the farthest bound  
    the ore in gloom and deep darkness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 4, 'They open shafts in a valley away from human habitation;  
    they are forgotten by travelers,  
    they sway suspended, remote from people.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 5, 'As for the earth, out of it comes bread;  
    but underneath it is turned up as by fire.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 6, 'Its stones are the place of sapphires,  
    and its dust contains gold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 7, '"That path no bird of prey knows,  
    and the falcon"s eye has not seen it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 8, 'The proud wild animals have not trodden it;  
    the lion has not passed over it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 9, '"They put their hand to the flinty rock,  
    and overturn mountains by the roots.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 10, 'They cut out channels in the rocks,  
    and their eyes see every precious thing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 11, 'The sources of the rivers they probe;  
    hidden things they bring to light.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 12, '"But where shall wisdom be found?  
    And where is the place of understanding?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 13, 'Mortals do not know the way to it,  
    and it is not found in the land of the living.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 14, 'The deep says, "It is not in me,"  
    and the sea says, "It is not with me."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 15, 'It cannot be gotten for gold,  
    and silver cannot be weighed out as its price.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 16, 'It cannot be valued in the gold of Ophir,  
    in precious onyx or sapphire.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 17, 'Gold and glass cannot equal it,  
    nor can it be exchanged for jewels of fine gold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 18, 'No mention shall be made of coral or of crystal;  
    the price of wisdom is above pearls.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 19, 'The chrysolite of Ethiopia cannot compare with it,  
    nor can it be valued in pure gold.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 20, '"Where then does wisdom come from?  
    And where is the place of understanding?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 21, 'It is hidden from the eyes of all living,  
    and concealed from the birds of the air.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 22, 'Abaddon and Death say,  
    "We have heard a rumor of it with our ears."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 23, '"God understands the way to it,  
    and he knows its place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 24, 'For he looks to the ends of the earth,  
    and sees everything under the heavens.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 25, 'When he gave to the wind its weight,  
    and apportioned out the waters by measure;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 26, 'when he made a decree for the rain,  
    and a way for the thunderbolt;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 27, 'then he saw it and declared it;  
    he established it, and searched it out.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 28, 28, 'And he said to humankind,  
"Truly, the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom;  
    and to depart from evil is understanding.""', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 1, 'Job again took up his discourse and said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Finishes His Defense'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 2, '"O that I were as in the months of old,  
    as in the days when God watched over me;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 3, 'when his lamp shone over my head,  
    and by his light I walked through darkness;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 4, 'when I was in my prime,  
    when the friendship of God was upon my tent;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 5, 'when the Almighty was still with me,  
    when my children were around me;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 6, 'when my steps were washed with milk,  
    and the rock poured out for me streams of oil!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 7, 'When I went out to the gate of the city,  
    when I took my seat in the square,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 8, 'the young men saw me and withdrew,  
    and the aged rose up and stood;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 9, 'the nobles refrained from talking,  
    and laid their hands on their mouths;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 10, 'the voices of princes were hushed,  
    and their tongues stuck to the roof of their mouths.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 11, 'When the ear heard, it commended me,  
    and when the eye saw, it approved;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 12, 'because I delivered the poor who cried,  
    and the orphan who had no helper.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 13, 'The blessing of the wretched came upon me,  
    and I caused the widow"s heart to sing for joy.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 14, 'I put on righteousness, and it clothed me;  
    my justice was like a robe and a turban.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 15, 'I was eyes to the blind,  
    and feet to the lame.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 16, 'I was a father to the needy,  
    and I championed the cause of the stranger.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 17, 'I broke the fangs of the unrighteous,  
    and made them drop their prey from their teeth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 18, 'Then I thought, "I shall die in my nest,  
    and I shall multiply my days like the phoenix;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 19, 'my roots spread out to the waters,  
    with the dew all night on my branches;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 20, 'my glory was fresh with me,  
    and my bow ever new in my hand."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 21, '"They listened to me, and waited,  
    and kept silence for my counsel.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 22, 'After I spoke they did not speak again,  
    and my word dropped upon them like dew.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 23, 'They waited for me as for the rain;  
    they opened their mouths as for the spring rain.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 24, 'I smiled on them when they had no confidence;  
    and the light of my countenance they did not extinguish.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 29, 25, 'I chose their way, and sat as chief,  
    and I lived like a king among his troops,  
    like one who comforts mourners.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 1, '"But now they make sport of me,  
    those who are younger than I,  
whose fathers I would have disdained  
    to set with the dogs of my flock.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 2, 'What could I gain from the strength of their hands?  
    All their vigor is gone.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 3, 'Through want and hard hunger  
    they gnaw the dry and desolate ground,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 4, 'they pick mallow and the leaves of bushes,  
    and to warm themselves the roots of broom.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 5, 'They are driven out from society;  
    people shout after them as after a thief.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 6, 'In the gullies of wadis they must live,  
    in holes in the ground, and in the rocks.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 7, 'Among the bushes they bray;  
    under the nettles they huddle together.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 8, 'A senseless, disreputable brood,  
    they have been whipped out of the land.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 9, '"And now they mock me in song;  
    I am a byword to them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 10, 'They abhor me, they keep aloof from me;  
    they do not hesitate to spit at the sight of me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 11, 'Because God has loosed my bowstring and humbled me,  
    they have cast off restraint in my presence.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 12, 'On my right hand the rabble rise up;  
    they send me sprawling,  
    and build roads for my ruin.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 13, 'They break up my path,  
    they promote my calamity;  
    no one restrains them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 14, 'As through a wide breach they come;  
    amid the crash they roll on.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 15, 'Terrors are turned upon me;  
    my honor is pursued as by the wind,  
    and my prosperity has passed away like a cloud.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 16, '"And now my soul is poured out within me;  
    days of affliction have taken hold of me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 17, 'The night racks my bones,  
    and the pain that gnaws me takes no rest.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 18, 'With violence he seizes my garment;  
    he grasps me by the collar of my tunic.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 19, 'He has cast me into the mire,  
    and I have become like dust and ashes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 20, 'I cry to you and you do not answer me;  
    I stand, and you merely look at me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 21, 'You have turned cruel to me;  
    with the might of your hand you persecute me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 22, 'You lift me up on the wind, you make me ride on it,  
    and you toss me about in the roar of the storm.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 23, 'I know that you will bring me to death,  
    and to the house appointed for all living.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 24, '"Surely one does not turn against the needy,  
    when in disaster they cry for help.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 25, 'Did I not weep for those whose day was hard?  
    Was not my soul grieved for the poor?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 26, 'But when I looked for good, evil came;  
    and when I waited for light, darkness came.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 27, 'My inward parts are in turmoil, and are never still;  
    days of affliction come to meet me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 28, 'I go about in sunless gloom;  
    I stand up in the assembly and cry for help.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 29, 'I am a brother of jackals,  
    and a companion of ostriches.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 30, 'My skin turns black and falls from me,  
    and my bones burn with heat.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 30, 31, 'My lyre is turned to mourning,  
    and my pipe to the voice of those who weep.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 1, '"I have made a covenant with my eyes;  
    how then could I look upon a virgin?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 2, 'What would be my portion from God above,  
    and my heritage from the Almighty on high?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 3, 'Does not calamity befall the unrighteous,  
    and disaster the workers of iniquity?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 4, 'Does he not see my ways,  
    and number all my steps?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 5, '"If I have walked with falsehood,  
    and my foot has hurried to deceit--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 6, 'let me be weighed in a just balance,  
    and let God know my integrity!--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 7, 'if my step has turned aside from the way,  
    and my heart has followed my eyes,  
    and if any spot has clung to my hands;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 8, 'then let me sow, and another eat;  
    and let what grows for me be rooted out.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 9, '"If my heart has been enticed by a woman,  
    and I have lain in wait at my neighbor"s door;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 10, 'then let my wife grind for another,  
    and let other men kneel over her.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 11, 'For that would be a heinous crime;  
    that would be a criminal offense;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 12, 'for that would be a fire consuming down to Abaddon,  
    and it would burn to the root all my harvest.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 13, '"If I have rejected the cause of my male or female slaves,  
    when they brought a complaint against me;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 14, 'what then shall I do when God rises up?  
    When he makes inquiry, what shall I answer him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 15, 'Did not he who made me in the womb make them?  
    And did not one fashion us in the womb?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 16, '"If I have withheld anything that the poor desired,  
    or have caused the eyes of the widow to fail,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 17, 'or have eaten my morsel alone,  
    and the orphan has not eaten from it--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 18, 'for from my youth I reared the orphan like a father,  
    and from my mother"s womb I guided the widow--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 19, 'if I have seen anyone perish for lack of clothing,  
    or a poor person without covering,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 20, 'whose loins have not blessed me,  
    and who was not warmed with the fleece of my sheep;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 21, 'if I have raised my hand against the orphan,  
    because I saw I had supporters at the gate;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 22, 'then let my shoulder blade fall from my shoulder,  
    and let my arm be broken from its socket.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 23, 'For I was in terror of calamity from God,  
    and I could not have faced his majesty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 24, '"If I have made gold my trust,  
    or called fine gold my confidence;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 25, 'if I have rejoiced because my wealth was great,  
    or because my hand had gotten much;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 26, 'if I have looked at the sun when it shone,  
    or the moon moving in splendor,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 27, 'and my heart has been secretly enticed,  
    and my mouth has kissed my hand;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 28, 'this also would be an iniquity to be punished by the judges,  
    for I should have been false to God above.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 29, '"If I have rejoiced at the ruin of those who hated me,  
    or exulted when evil overtook them--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 30, 'I have not let my mouth sin  
    by asking for their lives with a curse--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 31, 'if those of my tent ever said,  
    "O that we might be sated with his flesh!"--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 32, 'the stranger has not lodged in the street;  
    I have opened my doors to the traveler--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 33, 'if I have concealed my transgressions as others do,  
    by hiding my iniquity in my bosom,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 34, 'because I stood in great fear of the multitude,  
    and the contempt of families terrified me,  
    so that I kept silence, and did not go out of doors--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 35, 'O that I had one to hear me!  
    (Here is my signature! Let the Almighty answer me!)  
    O that I had the indictment written by my adversary!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 36, 'Surely I would carry it on my shoulder;  
    I would bind it on me like a crown;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 37, 'I would give him an account of all my steps;  
    like a prince I would approach him.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 38, '"If my land has cried out against me,  
    and its furrows have wept together;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 39, 'if I have eaten its yield without payment,  
    and caused the death of its owners;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 31, 40, 'let thorns grow instead of wheat,  
    and foul weeds instead of barley."
The words of Job are ended.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 1, 'So these three men ceased to answer Job, because he was righteous in his own eyes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Rebukes Job"s Friends'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 2, 'Then Elihu son of Barachel the Buzite, of the family of Ram, became angry. He was angry at Job because he justified himself rather than God;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 3, 'he was angry also at Job"s three friends because they had found no answer, though they had declared Job to be in the wrong.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 4, 'Now Elihu had waited to speak to Job, because they were older than he.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 5, 'But when Elihu saw that there was no answer in the mouths of these three men, he became angry.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 6, 'Elihu son of Barachel the Buzite answered:
"I am young in years,  
    and you are aged;  
therefore I was timid and afraid  
    to declare my opinion to you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 7, 'I said, "Let days speak,  
    and many years teach wisdom."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 8, 'But truly it is the spirit in a mortal,  
    the breath of the Almighty, that makes for understanding.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 9, 'It is not the old that are wise,  
    nor the aged that understand what is right.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 10, 'Therefore I say, "Listen to me;  
    let me also declare my opinion."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 11, '"See, I waited for your words,  
    I listened for your wise sayings,  
    while you searched out what to say.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 12, 'I gave you my attention,  
    but there was in fact no one that confuted Job,  
    no one among you that answered his words.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 13, 'Yet do not say, "We have found wisdom;  
    God may vanquish him, not a human."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 14, 'He has not directed his words against me,  
    and I will not answer him with your speeches.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 15, '"They are dismayed, they answer no more;  
    they have not a word to say.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 16, 'And am I to wait, because they do not speak,  
    because they stand there, and answer no more?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 17, 'I also will give my answer;  
    I also will declare my opinion.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 18, 'For I am full of words;  
    the spirit within me constrains me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 19, 'My heart is indeed like wine that has no vent;  
    like new wineskins, it is ready to burst.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 20, 'I must speak, so that I may find relief;  
    I must open my lips and answer.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 21, 'I will not show partiality to any person  
    or use flattery toward anyone.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 32, 22, 'For I do not know how to flatter--  
    or my Maker would soon put an end to me!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 1, '"But now, hear my speech, O Job,  
    and listen to all my words.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Rebukes Job'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 2, 'See, I open my mouth;  
    the tongue in my mouth speaks.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 3, 'My words declare the uprightness of my heart,  
    and what my lips know they speak sincerely.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 4, 'The spirit of God has made me,  
    and the breath of the Almighty gives me life.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 5, 'Answer me, if you can;  
    set your words in order before me; take your stand.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 6, 'See, before God I am as you are;  
    I too was formed from a piece of clay.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 7, 'No fear of me need terrify you;  
    my pressure will not be heavy on you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 8, '"Surely, you have spoken in my hearing,  
    and I have heard the sound of your words.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 9, 'You say, "I am clean, without transgression;  
    I am pure, and there is no iniquity in me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 10, 'Look, he finds occasions against me,  
    he counts me as his enemy;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 11, 'he puts my feet in the stocks,  
    and watches all my paths."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 12, '"But in this you are not right. I will answer you:  
    God is greater than any mortal.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 13, 'Why do you contend against him,  
    saying, "He will answer none of my words"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 14, 'For God speaks in one way,  
    and in two, though people do not perceive it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 15, 'In a dream, in a vision of the night,  
    when deep sleep falls on mortals,  
    while they slumber on their beds,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 16, 'then he opens their ears,  
    and terrifies them with warnings,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 17, 'that he may turn them aside from their deeds,  
    and keep them from pride,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 18, 'to spare their souls from the Pit,  
    their lives from traversing the River.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 19, 'They are also chastened with pain upon their beds,  
    and with continual strife in their bones,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 20, 'so that their lives loathe bread,  
    and their appetites dainty food.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 21, 'Their flesh is so wasted away that it cannot be seen;  
    and their bones, once invisible, now stick out.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 22, 'Their souls draw near the Pit,  
    and their lives to those who bring death.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 23, 'Then, if there should be for one of them an angel,  
    a mediator, one of a thousand,  
    one who declares a person upright,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 24, 'and he is gracious to that person, and says,  
    "Deliver him from going down into the Pit;  
    I have found a ransom;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 25, 'let his flesh become fresh with youth;  
    let him return to the days of his youthful vigor";', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 26, 'then he prays to God, and is accepted by him,  
    he comes into his presence with joy,  
and God repays him for his righteousness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 27, 'That person sings to others and says,  
"I sinned, and perverted what was right,  
    and it was not paid back to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 28, 'He has redeemed my soul from going down to the Pit,  
    and my life shall see the light."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 29, '"God indeed does all these things,  
    twice, three times, with mortals,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 30, 'to bring back their souls from the Pit,  
    so that they may see the light of life.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 31, 'Pay heed, Job, listen to me;  
    be silent, and I will speak.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 32, 'If you have anything to say, answer me;  
    speak, for I desire to justify you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 33, 33, 'If not, listen to me;  
    be silent, and I will teach you wisdom."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 1, 'Then Elihu continued and said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Proclaims God"s Justice'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 2, '"Hear my words, you wise men,  
    and give ear to me, you who know;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 3, 'for the ear tests words  
    as the palate tastes food.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 4, 'Let us choose what is right;  
    let us determine among ourselves what is good.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 5, 'For Job has said, "I am innocent,  
    and God has taken away my right;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 6, 'in spite of being right I am counted a liar;  
    my wound is incurable, though I am without transgression."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 7, 'Who is there like Job,  
    who drinks up scoffing like water,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 8, 'who goes in company with evildoers  
    and walks with the wicked?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 9, 'For he has said, "It profits one nothing  
    to take delight in God."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 10, '"Therefore, hear me, you who have sense,  
    far be it from God that he should do wickedness,  
    and from the Almighty that he should do wrong.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 11, 'For according to their deeds he will repay them,  
    and according to their ways he will make it befall them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 12, 'Of a truth, God will not do wickedly,  
    and the Almighty will not pervert justice.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 13, 'Who gave him charge over the earth  
    and who laid on him the whole world?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 14, 'If he should take back his spirit to himself,  
    and gather to himself his breath,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 15, 'all flesh would perish together,  
    and all mortals return to dust.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 16, '"If you have understanding, hear this;  
    listen to what I say.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 17, 'Shall one who hates justice govern?  
    Will you condemn one who is righteous and mighty,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 18, 'who says to a king, "You scoundrel!"  
    and to princes, "You wicked men!";', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 19, 'who shows no partiality to nobles,  
    nor regards the rich more than the poor,  
    for they are all the work of his hands?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 20, 'In a moment they die;  
    at midnight the people are shaken and pass away,  
    and the mighty are taken away by no human hand.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 21, '"For his eyes are upon the ways of mortals,  
    and he sees all their steps.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 22, 'There is no gloom or deep darkness  
    where evildoers may hide themselves.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 23, 'For he has not appointed a time for anyone  
    to go before God in judgment.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 24, 'He shatters the mighty without investigation,  
    and sets others in their place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 25, 'Thus, knowing their works,  
    he overturns them in the night, and they are crushed.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 26, 'He strikes them for their wickedness  
    while others look on,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 27, 'because they turned aside from following him,  
    and had no regard for any of his ways,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 28, 'so that they caused the cry of the poor to come to him,  
    and he heard the cry of the afflicted--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 29, 'When he is quiet, who can condemn?  
    When he hides his face, who can behold him,  
    whether it be a nation or an individual?--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 30, 'so that the godless should not reign,  
    or those who ensnare the people.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 31, '"For has anyone said to God,  
    "I have endured punishment; I will not offend any more;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 32, 'teach me what I do not see;  
    if I have done iniquity, I will do it no more"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 33, 'Will he then pay back to suit you,  
    because you reject it?  
For you must choose, and not I;  
    therefore declare what you know.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 34, 'Those who have sense will say to me,  
    and the wise who hear me will say,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 35, '"Job speaks without knowledge,  
    his words are without insight."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 36, 'Would that Job were tried to the limit,  
    because his answers are those of the wicked.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 34, 37, 'For he adds rebellion to his sin;  
    he claps his hands among us,  
    and multiplies his words against God."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 1, 'Elihu continued and said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Condemns Self-Righteousness'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 2, '"Do you think this to be just?  
    You say, "I am in the right before God."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 3, 'If you ask, "What advantage have I?  
    How am I better off than if I had sinned?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 4, 'I will answer you  
    and your friends with you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 5, 'Look at the heavens and see;  
    observe the clouds, which are higher than you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 6, 'If you have sinned, what do you accomplish against him?  
    And if your transgressions are multiplied, what do you do to him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 7, 'If you are righteous, what do you give to him;  
    or what does he receive from your hand?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 8, 'Your wickedness affects others like you,  
    and your righteousness, other human beings.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 9, '"Because of the multitude of oppressions people cry out;  
    they call for help because of the arm of the mighty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 10, 'But no one says, "Where is God my Maker,  
    who gives strength in the night,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 11, 'who teaches us more than the animals of the earth,  
    and makes us wiser than the birds of the air?"', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 12, 'There they cry out, but he does not answer,  
    because of the pride of evildoers.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 13, 'Surely God does not hear an empty cry,  
    nor does the Almighty regard it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 14, 'How much less when you say that you do not see him,  
    that the case is before him, and you are waiting for him!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 15, 'And now, because his anger does not punish,  
    and he does not greatly heed transgression,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 35, 16, 'Job opens his mouth in empty talk,  
    he multiplies words without knowledge."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 1, 'Elihu continued and said:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Exalts God"s Goodness'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 2, '"Bear with me a little, and I will show you,  
    for I have yet something to say on God"s behalf.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 3, 'I will bring my knowledge from far away,  
    and ascribe righteousness to my Maker.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 4, 'For truly my words are not false;  
    one who is perfect in knowledge is with you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 5, '"Surely God is mighty and does not despise any;  
    he is mighty in strength of understanding.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 6, 'He does not keep the wicked alive,  
    but gives the afflicted their right.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 7, 'He does not withdraw his eyes from the righteous,  
    but with kings on the throne  
    he sets them forever, and they are exalted.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 8, 'And if they are bound in fetters  
    and caught in the cords of affliction,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 9, 'then he declares to them their work  
    and their transgressions, that they are behaving arrogantly.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 10, 'He opens their ears to instruction,  
    and commands that they return from iniquity.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 11, 'If they listen, and serve him,  
    they complete their days in prosperity,  
    and their years in pleasantness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 12, 'But if they do not listen, they shall perish by the sword,  
    and die without knowledge.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 13, '"The godless in heart cherish anger;  
    they do not cry for help when he binds them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 14, 'They die in their youth,  
    and their life ends in shame.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 15, 'He delivers the afflicted by their affliction,  
    and opens their ear by adversity.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 16, 'He also allured you out of distress  
    into a broad place where there was no constraint,  
    and what was set on your table was full of fatness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 17, '"But you are obsessed with the case of the wicked;  
    judgment and justice seize you.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 18, 'Beware that wrath does not entice you into scoffing,  
    and do not let the greatness of the ransom turn you aside.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 19, 'Will your cry avail to keep you from distress,  
    or will all the force of your strength?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 20, 'Do not long for the night,  
    when peoples are cut off in their place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 21, 'Beware! Do not turn to iniquity;  
    because of that you have been tried by affliction.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 22, 'See, God is exalted in his power;  
    who is a teacher like him?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 23, 'Who has prescribed for him his way,  
    or who can say, "You have done wrong"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 24, '"Remember to extol his work,  
    of which mortals have sung.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Elihu Proclaims God"s Majesty'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 25, 'All people have looked on it;  
    everyone watches it from far away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 26, 'Surely God is great, and we do not know him;  
    the number of his years is unsearchable.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 27, 'For he draws up the drops of water;  
    he distills his mist in rain,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 28, 'which the skies pour down  
    and drop upon mortals abundantly.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 29, 'Can anyone understand the spreading of the clouds,  
    the thunderings of his pavilion?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 30, 'See, he scatters his lightning around him  
    and covers the roots of the sea.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 31, 'For by these he governs peoples;  
    he gives food in abundance.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 32, 'He covers his hands with the lightning,  
    and commands it to strike the mark.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 36, 33, 'Its crashing tells about him;  
    he is jealous with anger against iniquity.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 1, '"At this also my heart trembles,  
    and leaps out of its place.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 2, 'Listen, listen to the thunder of his voice  
    and the rumbling that comes from his mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 3, 'Under the whole heaven he lets it loose,  
    and his lightning to the corners of the earth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 4, 'After it his voice roars;  
    he thunders with his majestic voice  
    and he does not restrain the lightnings when his voice is heard.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 5, 'God thunders wondrously with his voice;  
    he does great things that we cannot comprehend.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 6, 'For to the snow he says, "Fall on the earth";  
    and the shower of rain, his heavy shower of rain,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 7, 'serves as a sign on everyone"s hand,  
    so that all whom he has made may know it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 8, 'Then the animals go into their lairs  
    and remain in their dens.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 9, 'From its chamber comes the whirlwind,  
    and cold from the scattering winds.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 10, 'By the breath of God ice is given,  
    and the broad waters are frozen fast.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 11, 'He loads the thick cloud with moisture;  
    the clouds scatter his lightning.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 12, 'They turn round and round by his guidance,  
    to accomplish all that he commands them  
    on the face of the habitable world.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 13, 'Whether for correction, or for his land,  
    or for love, he causes it to happen.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 14, '"Hear this, O Job;  
    stop and consider the wondrous works of God.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 15, 'Do you know how God lays his command upon them,  
    and causes the lightning of his cloud to shine?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 16, 'Do you know the balancings of the clouds,  
    the wondrous works of the one whose knowledge is perfect,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 17, 'you whose garments are hot  
    when the earth is still because of the south wind?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 18, 'Can you, like him, spread out the skies,  
    hard as a molten mirror?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 19, 'Teach us what we shall say to him;  
    we cannot draw up our case because of darkness.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 20, 'Should he be told that I want to speak?  
    Did anyone ever wish to be swallowed up?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 21, 'Now, no one can look on the light  
    when it is bright in the skies,  
    when the wind has passed and cleared them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 22, 'Out of the north comes golden splendor;  
    around God is awesome majesty.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 23, 'The Almighty--we cannot find him;  
    he is great in power and justice,  
    and abundant righteousness he will not violate.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 37, 24, 'Therefore mortals fear him;  
    he does not regard any who are wise in their own conceit."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 1, 'Then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'The LORD Answers Job'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 2, '"Who is this that darkens counsel by words without knowledge?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 3, 'Gird up your loins like a man,  
    I will question you, and you shall declare to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 4, '"Where were you when I laid the foundation of the earth?  
    Tell me, if you have understanding.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 5, 'Who determined its measurements--surely you know!  
    Or who stretched the line upon it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 6, 'On what were its bases sunk,  
    or who laid its cornerstone', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 7, 'when the morning stars sang together  
    and all the heavenly beings shouted for joy?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 8, '"Or who shut in the sea with doors  
    when it burst out from the womb?--', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 9, 'when I made the clouds its garment,  
    and thick darkness its swaddling band,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 10, 'and prescribed bounds for it,  
    and set bars and doors,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 11, 'and said, "Thus far shall you come, and no farther,  
    and here shall your proud waves be stopped"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 12, '"Have you commanded the morning since your days began,  
    and caused the dawn to know its place,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 13, 'so that it might take hold of the skirts of the earth,  
    and the wicked be shaken out of it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 14, 'It is changed like clay under the seal,  
    and it is dyed like a garment.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 15, 'Light is withheld from the wicked,  
    and their uplifted arm is broken.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 16, '"Have you entered into the springs of the sea,  
    or walked in the recesses of the deep?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 17, 'Have the gates of death been revealed to you,  
    or have you seen the gates of deep darkness?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 18, 'Have you comprehended the expanse of the earth?  
    Declare, if you know all this.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 19, '"Where is the way to the dwelling of light,  
    and where is the place of darkness,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 20, 'that you may take it to its territory  
    and that you may discern the paths to its home?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 21, 'Surely you know, for you were born then,  
    and the number of your days is great!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 22, '"Have you entered the storehouses of the snow,  
    or have you seen the storehouses of the hail,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 23, 'which I have reserved for the time of trouble,  
    for the day of battle and war?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 24, 'What is the way to the place where the light is distributed,  
    or where the east wind is scattered upon the earth?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 25, '"Who has cut a channel for the torrents of rain,  
    and a way for the thunderbolt,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 26, 'to bring rain on a land where no one lives,  
    on the desert, which is empty of human life,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 27, 'to satisfy the waste and desolate land,  
    and to make the ground put forth grass?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 28, '"Has the rain a father,  
    or who has begotten the drops of dew?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 29, 'From whose womb did the ice come forth,  
    and who has given birth to the hoarfrost of heaven?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 30, 'The waters become hard like stone,  
    and the face of the deep is frozen.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 31, '"Can you bind the chains of the Pleiades,  
    or loose the cords of Orion?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 32, 'Can you lead forth the Mazzaroth in their season,  
    or can you guide the Bear with its children?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 33, 'Do you know the ordinances of the heavens?  
    Can you establish their rule on the earth?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 34, '"Can you lift up your voice to the clouds,  
    so that a flood of waters may cover you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 35, 'Can you send forth lightnings, so that they may go  
    and say to you, "Here we are"?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 36, 'Who has put wisdom in the inward parts,  
    or given understanding to the mind?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 37, 'Who has the wisdom to number the clouds?  
    Or who can tilt the waterskins of the heavens,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 38, 'when the dust runs into a mass  
    and the clods cling together?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 39, '"Can you hunt the prey for the lion,  
    or satisfy the appetite of the young lions,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 40, 'when they crouch in their dens,  
    or lie in wait in their covert?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 38, 41, 'Who provides for the raven its prey,  
    when its young ones cry to God,  
    and wander about for lack of food?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 1, '"Do you know when the mountain goats give birth?  
    Do you observe the calving of the deer?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 2, 'Can you number the months that they fulfill,  
    and do you know the time when they give birth,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 3, 'when they crouch to give birth to their offspring,  
    and are delivered of their young?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 4, 'Their young ones become strong, they grow up in the open;  
    they go forth, and do not return to them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 5, '"Who has let the wild ass go free?  
    Who has loosed the bonds of the swift ass,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 6, 'to which I have given the steppe for its home,  
    the salt land for its dwelling place?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 7, 'It scorns the tumult of the city;  
    it does not hear the shouts of the driver.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 8, 'It ranges the mountains as its pasture,  
    and it searches after every green thing.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 9, '"Is the wild ox willing to serve you?  
    Will it spend the night at your crib?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 10, 'Can you tie it in the furrow with ropes,  
    or will it harrow the valleys after you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 11, 'Will you depend on it because its strength is great,  
    and will you hand over your labor to it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 12, 'Do you have faith in it that it will return,  
    and bring your grain to your threshing floor?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 13, '"The ostrich"s wings flap wildly,  
    though its pinions lack plumage.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 14, 'For it leaves its eggs to the earth,  
    and lets them be warmed on the ground,', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 15, 'forgetting that a foot may crush them,  
    and that a wild animal may trample them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 16, 'It deals cruelly with its young, as if they were not its own;  
    though its labor should be in vain, yet it has no fear;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 17, 'because God has made it forget wisdom,  
    and given it no share in understanding.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 18, 'When it spreads its plumes aloft,  
    it laughs at the horse and its rider.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 19, '"Do you give the horse its might?  
    Do you clothe its neck with mane?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 20, 'Do you make it leap like the locust?  
    Its majestic snorting is terrible.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 21, 'It paws violently, exults mightily;  
    it goes out to meet the weapons.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 22, 'It laughs at fear, and is not dismayed;  
    it does not turn back from the sword.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 23, 'Upon it rattle the quiver,  
    the flashing spear, and the javelin.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 24, 'With fierceness and rage it swallows the ground;  
    it cannot stand still at the sound of the trumpet.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 25, 'When the trumpet sounds, it says "Aha!"  
    From a distance it smells the battle,  
    the thunder of the captains, and the shouting.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 26, '"Is it by your wisdom that the hawk soars,  
    and spreads its wings toward the south?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 27, 'Is it at your command that the eagle mounts up  
    and makes its nest on high?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 28, 'It lives on the rock and makes its home  
    in the fastness of the rocky crag.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 29, 'From there it spies the prey;  
    its eyes see it from far away.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 39, 30, 'Its young ones suck up blood;  
    and where the slain are, there it is."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 1, 'And the LORD said to Job:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 2, '"Shall a faultfinder contend with the Almighty?  
    Anyone who argues with God must respond."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 3, 'Then Job answered the LORD:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job"s Response to God'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 4, '"See, I am of small account; what shall I answer you?  
    I lay my hand on my mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 5, 'I have spoken once, and I will not answer;  
    twice, but will proceed no further."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 6, 'Then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'God"s Challenge to Job'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 7, '"Gird up your loins like a man;  
    I will question you, and you declare to me.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 8, 'Will you even put me in the wrong?  
    Will you condemn me that you may be justified?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 9, 'Have you an arm like God,  
    and can you thunder with a voice like his?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 10, '"Deck yourself with majesty and dignity;  
    clothe yourself with glory and splendor.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 11, 'Pour out the overflowings of your anger,  
    and look on all who are proud, and abase them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 12, 'Look on all who are proud, and bring them low;  
    tread down the wicked where they stand.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 13, 'Hide them all in the dust together;  
    bind their faces in the world below.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 14, 'Then I will also acknowledge to you  
    that your own right hand can give you victory.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 15, '"Look at Behemoth,  
    which I made just as I made you;  
    it eats grass like an ox.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 16, 'Its strength is in its loins,  
    and its power in the muscles of its belly.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 17, 'It makes its tail stiff like a cedar;  
    the sinews of its thighs are knit together.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 18, 'Its bones are tubes of bronze,  
    its limbs like bars of iron.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 19, '"It is the first of the great acts of God--  
    only its Maker can approach it with the sword.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 20, 'For the mountains yield food for it  
    where all the wild animals play.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 21, 'Under the lotus plants it lies,  
    in the covert of the reeds and in the marsh.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 22, 'The lotus trees cover it for shade;  
    the willows of the wadi surround it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 23, 'Even if the river is turbulent, it is not frightened;  
    it is confident though Jordan rushes against its mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 40, 24, 'Can one take it with hooks  
    or pierce its nose with a snare?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 1, '"Can you draw out Leviathan with a fishhook,  
    or press down its tongue with a cord?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 2, 'Can you put a rope in its nose,  
    or pierce its jaw with a hook?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 3, 'Will it make many supplications to you?  
    Will it speak soft words to you?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 4, 'Will it make a covenant with you  
    to be taken as your servant forever?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 5, 'Will you play with it as with a bird,  
    or will you put it on leash for your girls?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 6, 'Will traders bargain over it?  
    Will they divide it up among the merchants?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 7, 'Can you fill its skin with harpoons,  
    or its head with fishing spears?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 8, 'Lay hands on it;  
    think of the battle; you will not do it again!', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 9, 'Any hope of capturing it will be disappointed;  
    were not even the gods overwhelmed at the sight of it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 10, 'No one is so fierce as to dare to stir it up.  
    Who can stand before it?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 11, 'Who can confront it and be safe?  
    --under the whole heaven, who?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 12, '"I will not keep silence concerning its limbs,  
    or its mighty strength, or its splendid frame.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 13, 'Who can strip off its outer garment?  
    Who can penetrate its double coat of mail?', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 14, 'Who can open the doors of its face?  
    There is terror all around its teeth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 15, 'Its back is made of shields in rows,  
    shut up closely as with a seal.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 16, 'One is so near to another  
    that no air can come between them.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 17, 'They are joined one to another;  
    they clasp each other and cannot be separated.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 18, 'Its sneezes flash forth light,  
    and its eyes are like the eyelids of the dawn.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 19, 'From its mouth go flaming torches;  
    sparks of fire leap out.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 20, 'Out of its nostrils comes smoke,  
    as from a boiling pot and burning rushes.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 21, 'Its breath kindles coals,  
    and a flame comes out of its mouth.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 22, 'In its neck abides strength,  
    and terror dances before it.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 23, 'The folds of its flesh cling together;  
    it is firmly cast and immovable.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 24, 'Its heart is as hard as stone,  
    as hard as the lower millstone.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 25, 'When it raises itself up the gods are afraid;  
    at the crashing they are beside themselves.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 26, 'Though the sword reaches it, it does not avail,  
    nor does the spear, the dart, or the javelin.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 27, 'It counts iron as straw,  
    and bronze as rotten wood.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 28, 'The arrow cannot make it flee;  
    slingstones, for it, are turned to chaff.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 29, 'Clubs are counted as chaff;  
    it laughs at the rattle of javelins.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 30, 'Its underparts are like sharp potsherds;  
    it spreads itself like a threshing sledge on the mire.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 31, 'It makes the deep boil like a pot;  
    it makes the sea like a pot of ointment.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 32, 'It leaves a shining wake behind it;  
    one would think the deep to be white-haired.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 33, 'On earth it has no equal,  
    a creature without fear.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 41, 34, 'It surveys everything that is lofty;  
    it is king over all that are proud."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

INSERT INTO public.bible_verses (book_no, book_abbr, book_name, chapter, verse, content, language, translation, title) VALUES
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 1, 'Then Job answered the LORD:', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job Is Humbled and Satisfied'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 2, '"I know that you can do all things,  
    and that no purpose of yours can be thwarted.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 3, '"Who is this that hides counsel without knowledge?"  
Therefore I have uttered what I did not understand,  
    things too wonderful for me, which I did not know.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 4, '"Hear, and I will speak;  
    I will question you, and you declare to me."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 5, 'I had heard of you by the hearing of the ear,  
    but now my eye sees you;', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 6, 'therefore I despise myself,  
    and repent in dust and ashes."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 7, 'After the LORD had spoken these words to Job, the LORD said to Eliphaz the Temanite: "My wrath is kindled against you and against your two friends; for you have not spoken of me what is right, as my servant Job has.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job"s Friends Are Humiliated'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 8, 'Now therefore take seven bulls and seven rams, and go to my servant Job, and offer up for yourselves a burnt offering; and my servant Job shall pray for you, for I will accept his prayer not to deal with you according to your folly; for you have not spoken of me what is right, as my servant Job has done."', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 9, 'So Eliphaz the Temanite and Bildad the Shuhite and Zophar the Naamathite went and did what the LORD had told them; and the LORD accepted Job"s prayer.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 10, 'And the LORD restored the fortunes of Job when he had prayed for his friends; and the LORD gave Job twice as much as he had before.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', 'Job"s Fortunes Are Restored Twofold'),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 11, 'Then there came to him all his brothers and sisters and all who had known him before, and they ate bread with him in his house; they showed him sympathy and comforted him for all the evil that the LORD had brought upon him; and each of them gave him a piece of money and a gold ring.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 12, 'The LORD blessed the latter days of Job more than his beginning; and he had fourteen thousand sheep, six thousand camels, a thousand yoke of oxen, and a thousand donkeys.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 13, 'He also had seven sons and three daughters.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 14, 'He named the first Jemimah, the second Keziah, and the third Keren-happuch.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 15, 'In all the land there were no women so beautiful as Job"s daughters; and their father gave them an inheritance along with their brothers.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 16, 'After this Job lived one hundred and forty years, and saw his children, and his children"s children, four generations.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL),
(18, 'Ayb', 'Job', 42, 17, 'And Job died, old and full of days.', 'en', 'NRSV-CE', NULL);

